OAI_PAPER({"slug": "Randomized-quasipolynomial-time-mean-payoff-games-September-25-2026", "title": "Randomized quasipolynomial-time mean-payoff games", "pdf": "https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/Randomized-quasipolynomial-time-mean-payoff-games-September-25-2026/paper.pdf", "family": "104", "family_title": "Quasipolynomial algorithms for mean-payoff, stochastic and parity games", "abstract_html": "We give a randomized algorithm that computes the complete zero-threshold winning set of a finite mean-payoff game with arbitrary signed integer edge weights encoded in binary. For total explicit input length <i>L</i>, it uses \\(2^{O((\\log(L+2))^2)}\\) bit operations on every random tape and is correct with probability at least 7/8. A polynomial-time check certifies the winning regions and positional strategies for both players or reports failure. Independent repetition therefore gives an always-correct algorithm with the same expected quasipolynomial bit bound.", "blocks": [{"label": "Theorem 1", "orig": "Theorem 1.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(G=(V,E)\\) be a finite directed graph with \\(V=\\{1,\\ldots,n\\}\\), \\(n\\ge 1\\), and no sinks. Self-loops and parallel edges are permitted.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(V=V_{\\mathrm{Max}}\\mathbin{\\dot\\cup}V_{\\mathrm{Min}}\\), and let \\(w:E\\to\\mathbb Z\\).</p><p class=\"li\">At a vertex, its owner chooses the next edge. Strategies may depend on the entire finite history.</p><p class=\"li\">For a play \\(\\pi=(e_0,e_1,\\ldots)\\), define\n  \\[\n  \\operatorname{MP}_w(\\pi)\n  =\n  \\liminf_{T\\to\\infty}\\frac1T\\sum_{j=0}^{T-1}w(e_j).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(L\\) be the total length of an explicit binary encoding of the graph, ownership, and signed weights; endpoints and ownership are included in this length.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a uniform randomized algorithm returning the entire set\n\\[\nU=\n\\left\\{\nv\\in V:\n\\exists\\text{ a Max strategy }\\sigma\\\n\\forall\\text{ Min strategies }\\tau,\\\n\\operatorname{MP}_w(\\pi_{v,\\sigma,\\tau})\\ge 0\n\\right\\}\n\\]\ncorrectly with probability at least \\(7/8\\). For an absolute constant \\(C&gt;0\\), on every random tape its total cost is at most\n\\[\n2^{C(\\log_2(L+2))^2}\n\\]\nbit operations in a standard model polynomially equivalent to a uniform probabilistic Turing machine. The bound includes preprocessing, recursive calls, intermediate arithmetic, and random-bit generation.</p>", "proof": "<p>From the explicit input compute \\(t,W,B_*\\) as in Proposition 1 and initialize the global parameters of Proposition 3. Run\n\\[\np=\\operatorname{Solve}(0,B_*;B_*,\\mathbf1,\\mathbf1,n,n),\n\\]\nand return\n\\[\nU_p=\\{i:p_i&gt;B_*/2\\}.\n\\]</p><p>Proposition 1 gives the unique deterministic solution \\(x\\) of \\([0,B_*]\\). Its supports have size at most \\(n\\), so it qualifies under both root budgets. Proposition 6 gives\n\\[\n\\Pr(p=x)\\ge7/8.\n\\]\nOn that single whole-vector event, Proposition 1 identifies \\(U_p\\) with the complete zero-threshold winning set for the original weights and liminf payoff, against arbitrary opposing history-dependent strategies.</p><p>Proposition 3 proves termination on every tape. Proposition 7 gives the full bit bound, including preprocessing and the \\(n\\) final comparisons. The routines use uniform finite instructions on binary integers, exact rationals, and fair bits. These give the claimed output probability and running-time bound.</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 2", "orig": "Corollary 8.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(G=(V,E)\\), \\(V=V_{\\mathrm{Max}}\\mathbin{\\dot\\cup}V_{\\mathrm{Min}}\\), \\(w:E\\to\\mathbb Z\\), \\(n\\), \\(L\\), and the mean-payoff objective be as in Theorem 1.</p><p class=\"li\">A positional strategy chooses one outgoing edge at each vertex owned by its player.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a randomized algorithm which, on every random tape, uses\n\\[\n2^{O((\\log_2(L+2))^2)}\n\\]\nbit operations and returns either a failure symbol or the exact winning set together with an integer potential and positional strategies for both players.</p><p>Whenever it returns these objects:</p><p class=\"li\">the Max strategy guarantees mean payoff at least zero from every vertex of the winning set;</p><p class=\"li\">the Min strategy ensures upper limiting average at most \\(-1/n\\) from every other vertex, against arbitrary opposing strategies.</p><p>It returns these objects with probability at least \\(7/8\\).</p><p>Repeating independent trials until a certificate is accepted terminates almost surely, never returns an incorrect answer, and has expected bit cost\n\\[\n2^{O((\\log_2(L+2))^2)}.\n\\]\nNo running-time bound on every tape is asserted for this repeated procedure; each individual trial retains that bound.</p>", "proof": "<p>Run the root solver to obtain \\(p\\in[0,B_*]\\), and check\n\\[\np=\\operatorname{clip}_{[0,B_*]}F(p).\n\\]\nAccept precisely when this equality holds. By Proposition 1 it holds if and only if \\(p=x\\), the unique top-box solution. Acceptance therefore certifies every coordinate. Proposition 6 gives acceptance probability at least \\(7/8\\).</p><p>Upon acceptance, form\n\\[\nU=\\{i:p_i&gt;B_*/2\\}.\n\\]\nAt each Max vertex in \\(U\\), choose an outgoing edge attaining \\(F_i(p)\\). At each Min vertex outside \\(U\\), choose an attaining edge as well. Complete the strategies arbitrarily at their other vertices. The two-band argument in Proposition 1 applies to the verified vector \\(p=x\\) and proves both strategy guarantees.</p><p>The check and edge choices scan the explicit edge list and use \\(O(L+1)\\)-bit integers. They add polynomial cost to the bounded-time trial of Proposition 7. Rejection returns the failure symbol, so no incorrect answer is accepted.</p><p>For independent repetitions, the probability that the first \\(r\\) trials reject is at most \\(8^{-r}\\). Acceptance therefore occurs almost surely, and the expected number of trials is at most\n\\[\n\\sum_{r\\ge0}8^{-r}=\\frac87.\n\\]\nMultiplying by the worst-case cost of one trial gives the asserted expected quasipolynomial bit bound. The repeated procedure has no asserted time bound on every tape; its individual trials do.</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 3", "orig": "Appendix A, an independent, unnumbered counterexample", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Consider the minimization variable-selection and substitution rule printed in Laurent Truffet, “Substitution for minimizing/maximizing a tropical linear (fractional) programming,” arXiv:2603.26423v4, August 29, 2026.</p><p class=\"li\">Equation and definition numbers mentioned in this theorem’s proof refer to that cited version.</p><p class=\"li\">In ordinary arithmetic, consider\n  \\[\n  \\min x_1\n  \\quad\\text{subject to}\\quad\n  \\max(x_1,x_2)\\ge 0,\n  \\qquad\n  x_1\\ge \\max(x_2+1,-2).\n  \\tag{A.1}\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Homogenization replaces the constraints by\n  \\[\n  \\max(x_1,x_2)\\ge h,\n  \\qquad\n  x_1\\ge\\max(x_2+1,h-2),\n  \\]\n  and introduces the objective row \\(z\\ge x_1\\). Dehomogenization sets \\(h=0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Choose the search endpoints \\(\\mu=0\\) and \\(\\lambda=-4\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The true minimum in \\((A.1)\\) is zero. The stated rule forces the first saturation \\(x_2=h\\), which removes every optimum; the subsequent saturation gives \\(x_1=h+1\\), and hence objective value \\(1\\) after dehomogenization.</p><p>This counterexample concerns only the cited version and rule. It does not assess another variable-selection rule, a repaired algorithm, another version, or the distinct 2025 HAL/MSR max-atom proof discussed in the paper.</p>", "proof": "<p>The point \\((x_1,x_2)=(0,-1)\\) is feasible in \\((A.1)\\). Conversely, if a feasible point had \\(x_1&lt;0\\), the second constraint would give\n\\[\nx_2\\le x_1-1&lt;0,\n\\]\ncontradicting \\(\\max(x_1,x_2)\\ge0\\). Thus the true minimum is exactly zero.</p><p>The cited input conditions hold for the homogenized instance. Equation (5b) of the cited paper holds for both ordered pairs of constraint rows: each left side lacks an \\(h\\)-term, whereas the other row’s right side has one. Setting \\(x_1=x_2=0\\) and increasing \\(h\\) rules out the forbidden functional domination by any finite additive constant. Both variables have lower or upper occurrences, as required by Definition 4.2 and Equation (34).</p><p>The endpoints \\(\\mu=0,\\lambda=-4\\) include the finite optimum witness\n\\[\n(x_1,x_2,h)=(0,-1,0).\n\\]\nThey satisfy Equation (23b) for the only mixed lower function \\(\\max(x_2+1,h-2)\\), since\n\\[\n1+\\lambda=-3&lt;-2+\\mu=-2.\n\\]\nThe lower function \\(h\\) has no competing \\(x\\)-term.</p><p>Numerotation convention 4.1 gives index \\(0\\) to the objective row. The lower and upper occurrences in Equations (24), (32), and Definition 4.1 come only from constraint rows \\(1,\\ldots,m\\). Here they are\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c|c}\n\\text{variable}&amp;\\text{lower-bound rows}&amp;\\text{upper-bound rows}\\\\ \\hline\nx_1&amp;1,2&amp;\\varnothing\\\\\nx_2&amp;1&amp;2\n\\end{array}\n\\]\nso the objective row is not an additional upper occurrence of \\(x_1\\). The lower branch for \\(x_2\\) is \\(x_2\\ge h\\) in row \\(1\\), and its upper bound is \\(x_2\\le x_1-1\\) in row \\(2\\).</p><p>Equation (53a) makes the dominating-variable set exactly \\(\\{x_2\\}\\). Equation (53b) restricts eligible lower inequalities to that set, leaving the unique pair \\((1,2)\\). Theorem 4.3, Equation (47), and Section 4.2.3, Case 2.2.2, of the cited version prescribe saturation\n\\[\nx_2=h.\n\\]\nThere is no tie-breaking choice.</p><p>After substitution, the second row requires\n\\[\nx_1\\ge h+1.\n\\]\nThe next eligible saturation therefore sets \\(x_1=h+1\\). The stopping test in Equation (39) fails before each substitution. At both stages the objective still depends on \\(x_1\\), so the separate rule for an objective-independent switch to maximization does not apply. Normalizing \\(h=0\\) gives objective value \\(1\\), although the original minimum is zero.</p><p>The first substitution already removes every optimum: selecting a lower-bound disjunct on the basis of an upper occurrence of its variable does not preserve the optimum of these coupled constraints. The calculation is confined to the cited version and rule and is independent of the mean-payoff algorithm.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 1", "orig": "Lemmas 2.1, 2.3, 2.4 and Proposition 2.5; Definition 2.2 is included below", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix a finite mean-payoff game as in Theorem 1.</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  t(e)=(n+1)w(e)+1,\n  \\qquad\n  W=\\max\\left\\{1,\\max_{e\\in E}|t(e)|\\right\\},\n  \\qquad\n  B_*=8nW.\n  \\tag{2.1}\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">A simple directed cycle has no repeated vertex except its endpoints; a self-loop has length one.</p><p class=\"li\">All vector inequalities, maxima, and minima are coordinatewise. A scalar added to a vector is added in every coordinate.</p><p class=\"li\">For integer vectors \\(a\\le b\\), define\n  \\[\n  [a,b]=\\{p\\in\\mathbb Z^V:a\\le p\\le b\\},\n  \\qquad\n  (\\operatorname{clip}_{[a,b]}p)_i\n  =\n  \\min\\{b_i,\\max\\{a_i,p_i\\}\\}.\n  \\]\n  A positive integer \\(B\\) is a reference width when \\(b_i-a_i\\le B\\) for every \\(i\\).</p><p class=\"li\">On all of \\(\\mathbb Z^V\\), define\n  \\[\n  F_i(p)=\n  \\begin{cases}\n  \\displaystyle\\max_{e:i\\to j}(t(e)+p_j),&amp;i\\in V_{\\mathrm{Max}},\\\\{}[1mm]\n  \\displaystyle\\min_{e:i\\to j}(t(e)+p_j),&amp;i\\in V_{\\mathrm{Min}}.\n  \\end{cases}\n  \\tag{2.2}\n  \\]\n  Thus\n  \\[\n  p\\le q\\Longrightarrow F(p)\\le F(q),\n  \\qquad\n  F(p+c)=F(p)+c\\quad(c\\in\\mathbb Z).\n  \\tag{2.3}\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">A subsolution of \\([a,b]\\) is a full vector \\(y\\in[a,b]\\) satisfying\n  \\[\n  y_i\\le F_i(y)\\quad(i\\in S_y),\n  \\qquad\n  S_y=\\{i:y_i&gt;a_i\\}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">A supersolution is a full vector \\(z\\in[a,b]\\) satisfying\n  \\[\n  z_i\\ge F_i(z)\\quad(i\\in S_z),\n  \\qquad\n  S_z=\\{i:z_i&lt;b_i\\}.\n  \\]\n  At a collapsed coordinate \\(a_i=b_i\\), neither support contains \\(i\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p class=\"li num\">1. Every subsolution of a finite integer box lies below every supersolution of that box.</p><p class=\"li num\">2. Every finite integer box has a unique vector \\(x\\) that is both a subsolution and a supersolution. It is the unique fixed point of\n   \\[\n   T_{a,b}(p)=\\operatorname{clip}_{[a,b]}F(p).\n   \\]\n   The procedure \\(\\operatorname{Basic}(a,b)\\), which starts at \\(p=a\\), synchronously evaluates \\(q=T_{a,b}(p)\\), returns \\(p\\) if \\(q=p\\), and otherwise replaces \\(p\\) by \\(q\\), returns \\(x\\) after at most\n   \\[\n   1+\\sum_{i\\in V}(b_i-a_i)\\le 1+nB\n   \\]\n   operator evaluations.</p><p class=\"li num\">3. If \\(x\\) is the solution of the constant box \\([0,B_*]\\), then the exact zero-threshold winning set is\n   \\[\n   U=\\{i:x_i&gt;B_*/2\\}.\n   \\]\n   There is a positional Max strategy guaranteeing original mean payoff at least zero on \\(U\\). There is a positional Min strategy ensuring original upper limiting average at most \\(-1/n\\) outside \\(U\\), against arbitrary history-dependent Max strategies.</p>", "proof": "<p>The comparison and iteration assertions are Lemmas 1.2 and 1.3. It remains to prove the winning-set reduction, originally Proposition 2.5.</p><p>Let \\(x\\) solve \\([0,B_*]\\). At an interior coordinate, \\(x_i=F_i(x)\\), so an attaining edge is tight:\n\\[\nx_i=t(e)+x_j.\n\\]\nFollow tight edges while vertices remain interior. A repetition would give a simple zero-sum \\(t\\)-cycle, excluded by Lemma 1.1. A boundary is therefore reached within \\(n-1\\) edges. For a coordinate already on a boundary the path has length zero. Telescoping proves that every coordinate lies within \\(nW\\) of one boundary, giving the partition\n\\[\nV_{\\mathrm{lo}}=\\{i:0\\le x_i\\le nW\\},\n\\qquad\nV_{\\mathrm{hi}}=\\{i:7nW\\le x_i\\le8nW\\}.\n\\tag{2.5}\n\\]\nThe midpoint \\(B_*/2=4nW\\) separates the bands.</p><p>On the high band the subsolution inequality is active. At a high Max vertex choose an attaining edge; at a high Min vertex keep every edge. All permitted edges satisfy\n\\[\nx_i\\le t(e)+x_j.\n\\tag{2.6}\n\\]\nTheir heads must also be high: a low head would imply\n\\[\n7nW\\le x_i\\le t(e)+x_j\\le(n+1)W&lt;7nW\n\\]\nfor \\(n\\ge1\\). The selected Max edges, extended arbitrarily at low vertices, define a positional strategy preserving the high band against every Min choice.</p><p>Every simple cycle in this permitted graph has nonnegative \\(t\\)-sum by \\((2.6)\\), and therefore nonnegative \\(w\\)-sum by Lemma 1.1. Set \\(M=\\max_e|w(e)|\\). Decompose a length-\\(T\\) prefix of a consistent play into simple cycles and a residual walk by repeatedly removing the segment ending at the first repeated vertex. The residual walk has at most \\(n-1\\) edges, and the removed cycles have nonnegative original sums. The prefix weight is at least\n\\[\n-(n-1)M.\n\\]\nDividing by \\(T\\) and taking the lower limit proves Max’s assertion without restricting Min’s dependence on the past.</p><p>On the low band the supersolution inequality is active. At a low Min vertex choose an attaining edge, and at a low Max vertex keep all edges. These edges satisfy\n\\[\nx_i\\ge t(e)+x_j.\n\\tag{2.7}\n\\]\nA high head would give\n\\[\nnW\\ge x_i\\ge t(e)+x_j\\ge(7n-1)W&gt;nW,\n\\]\nso this permitted graph stays low. Extend the chosen Min edges arbitrarily elsewhere. Each permitted simple cycle has nonpositive \\(t\\)-sum and hence strictly negative integer \\(w\\)-sum, at most \\(-1\\).</p><p>In a decomposition of a length-\\(T\\) prefix into \\(r\\) simple cycles and a residual walk, cycle lengths are at most \\(n\\) and residual length at most \\(n-1\\). Consequently,\n\\[\nr\\ge\\frac{T-(n-1)}n,\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\sum_{j&lt;T}w(e_j)\n\\le-r+(n-1)M\n\\le-\\frac{T-(n-1)}n+(n-1)M.\n\\]\nThe upper limiting average is at most \\(-1/n\\). This holds for every play allowed by the chosen Min strategy, including history-dependent opposing play. The two bands prove the exact winning-set formula.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.1", "orig": "Lemma 2.1, “Cycle signs.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the transformed weights \\(t(e)=(n+1)w(e)+1\\) of Proposition 1.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Every simple directed cycle has nonzero \\(t\\)-sum. Its \\(t\\)-sum is positive exactly when its \\(w\\)-sum is nonnegative, and negative exactly when its \\(w\\)-sum is negative.</p>", "proof": "<p>For a cycle of length \\(\\ell\\in\\{1,\\ldots,n\\}\\) and integer \\(w\\)-sum \\(c\\), the new sum is\n\\[\n(n+1)c+\\ell.\n\\]\nIf \\(c\\ge 0\\), this is at least one. If \\(c&lt;0\\), then \\(c\\le-1\\), and the new sum is at most\n\\[\n-(n+1)+n=-1.\n\\]\nThe calculation concerns the actual edges of the cycle and therefore also covers parallel edges.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.2", "orig": "Lemma 2.3, “Comparison principle.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the transformed operator \\(F\\) of Proposition 1.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(a\\le b\\) be arbitrary integer vectors.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(y\\in[a,b]\\) be a subsolution and \\(z\\in[a,b]\\) a supersolution, with the support conventions in Proposition 1.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\ny_i\\le z_i\\qquad(i\\in V).\n\\]\nThis includes boxes with negative, nonconstant, or collapsed coordinate intervals.</p>", "proof": "<p>Suppose\n\\[\nd=\\max_i(y_i-z_i)&gt;0,\n\\]\nand take a vertex attaining this maximum. There\n\\[\ny_i&gt;z_i\\ge a_i,\n\\qquad\nz_i&lt;y_i\\le b_i,\n\\]\nso both defining inequalities apply. Since \\(y\\le z+d\\), monotonicity and translation give\n\\[\nd=y_i-z_i\\le F_i(y)-F_i(z)\\le d.\n\\]\nEquality forces both comparison slacks to vanish:\n\\[\ny_i=F_i(y),\n\\qquad\nz_i=F_i(z).\n\\tag{2.4}\n\\]</p><p>A common tight edge leads to another vertex attaining \\(d\\). At a Max vertex choose an edge \\(e:i\\to j\\) attaining \\(F_i(y)\\); at a Min vertex choose one attaining \\(F_i(z)\\). In either case,\n\\[\nd=F_i(y)-F_i(z)\n\\le(t(e)+y_j)-(t(e)+z_j)\n=y_j-z_j\n\\le d.\n\\]\nEvery inequality is an equality. The edge is tight for both vectors, and its head again attains the maximum difference.</p><p>Continue choosing edges in this way. Finiteness supplies a repeated vertex; stopping at the first repetition produces a simple cycle of edges satisfying\n\\[\ny_i=t(e)+y_j.\n\\]\nSumming gives a zero \\(t\\)-sum, contrary to Lemma 1.1. Thus no positive maximum difference exists.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.3", "orig": "Lemma 2.4, “The box solution and basic iteration.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the operator \\(F\\), finite integer box \\([a,b]\\), clipped operator \\(T_{a,b}\\), and procedure \\(\\operatorname{Basic}\\) of Proposition 1.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(B\\) be any positive integer reference width.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is exactly one vector \\(x\\in[a,b]\\) that is both a subsolution and a supersolution. It is exactly the unique fixed point of \\(T_{a,b}\\).</p><p>The procedure \\(\\operatorname{Basic}(a,b)\\) returns \\(x\\) after at most\n\\[\n1+\\sum_{i\\in V}(b_i-a_i)\\le1+nB\n\\]\noperator evaluations, including the final stability test. No positivity or nondegeneracy of the coordinate intervals is assumed.</p>", "proof": "<p>Inspect the fixed-point condition in one coordinate. In the strict interior it says \\(F_i(p)=p_i\\), the conjunction of the two comparison inequalities. At \\(p_i=a_i&lt;b_i\\), clipping fixes \\(p_i\\) exactly when \\(F_i(p)\\le p_i\\), the active supersolution inequality. At \\(a_i&lt;p_i=b_i\\), it fixes \\(p_i\\) exactly when \\(F_i(p)\\ge p_i\\), the active subsolution inequality. A collapsed coordinate is always fixed by clipping and imposes neither comparison. Fixed points are therefore precisely the vectors having both comparison properties.</p><p>The map \\(T_{a,b}\\) is monotone and takes the box into itself. Its first iterate is at least \\(a\\), so successive iterates from \\(a\\) are nondecreasing. Each strict update increases the integer sum of the coordinates by at least one, whereas the available total increase is\n\\[\n\\sum_i(b_i-a_i).\n\\]\nThis proves existence, termination, and the count, with one extra evaluation to recognize stability.</p><p>If \\(x,x'\\) are fixed points, Lemma 1.2 applied in both orders gives \\(x\\le x'\\) and \\(x'\\le x\\). Hence \\(x=x'\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 2", "orig": "Lemmas 3.2–3.6; Definition 3.1 and the rank definition are included below", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix the transformed operator \\(F\\) of Proposition 1 and a finite integer box \\([a,b]\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\alpha,\\beta\\in\\mathbb Q_{&gt;0}^V\\) and \\(k,m\\in\\mathbb Q_{&gt;0}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(S\\subseteq V\\), write\n  \\[\n  \\alpha(S)=\\sum_{i\\in S}\\alpha_i,\n  \\qquad\n  \\beta(S)=\\sum_{i\\in S}\\beta_i.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">A subsolution \\(y\\) is admissible when \\(\\alpha(S_y)\\le k\\); a supersolution \\(z\\) is admissible when \\(\\beta(S_z)\\le m\\). Supports are relative to the specified box.</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  R=km\\sum_{i\\in V}\\frac1{\\alpha_i\\beta_i},\n  \\qquad\n  \\mu(S)=\n  \\frac{\\sum_{i\\in S}1/(\\alpha_i\\beta_i)}\n       {\\sum_{i\\in V}1/(\\alpha_i\\beta_i)},\n  \\tag{3.2}\n  \\]\n  and\n  \\[\n  d(R)=\\min\\{d\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}:(9/10)^dR&lt;1\\}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Put \\(\\eta=4/5\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The following mechanisms preserve the indicated comparisons and provide rank decreases.</p><p class=\"li num\">1. If \\(R&lt;1\\), the endpoint vector\n   \\[\n   p_i=\n   \\begin{cases}\n   b_i,&amp;\\beta_i&gt;m,\\\\\n   a_i,&amp;\\beta_i\\le m\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\]\n   satisfies \\(y\\le p\\le z\\) for all admissible comparisons.</p><p class=\"li num\">2. Multiplying either budget by \\(\\eta\\) multiplies \\(R\\) by \\(\\eta\\). If \\(R\\ge1\\) and \\(R'\\le(9/10)R\\), then \\(d(R')\\le d(R)-1\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">3. For \\(H\\subseteq V\\) with \\(\\mu(H)\\le1/2\\), halving \\(\\alpha_i\\) on \\(H\\) and replacing \\(k\\) by \\(3k/5\\) preserves every admissible subsolution satisfying\n   \\[\n   \\alpha(S_y\\cap H)&gt;\\eta k\n   \\]\n   and preserves every admissible supersolution. The symmetric operation on \\(\\beta,m\\) preserves supersolutions satisfying \\(\\beta(S_z\\cap H)&gt;\\eta m\\), and all admissible subsolutions. In either case,\n   \\[\n   R'=\\frac35(1+\\mu(H))R\\le\\frac9{10}R.\n   \\]</p><p class=\"li num\">4. Raising a lower boundary projects a subsolution by a coordinatewise maximum; lowering an upper boundary projects a supersolution by a coordinatewise minimum. The centered projections of Lemma 2.4 also preserve comparisons and admissibility, and their supports are contained in the parent supports.</p><p class=\"li num\">5. For a reference width \\(B&gt;128s\\), where \\(s\\ge1\\) is an integer, set\n   \\[\n   h=1/s,\\qquad\n   \\delta=\\lfloor B/(4s)\\rfloor,\\qquad\n   C=B-\\delta.\n   \\]\n   A subsolution with \\(\\max_i(y_i-a_i)&gt;C\\) has a translated-and-clipped subsolution \\(u\\le y\\), with maximum height \\(\\delta\\) above \\(a\\), supported inside\n   \\[\n   \\{i:y_i\\ge a_i+B-hB\\}.\n   \\]\n   Dually, a supersolution with \\(\\max_i(b_i-z_i)&gt;C\\) has a supersolution \\(v\\ge z\\), with maximum distance \\(\\delta\\) below \\(b\\), supported inside\n   \\[\n   \\{i:z_i\\le b_i-B+hB\\}.\n   \\]\n   If these respective extreme sets have weight at most the reduced budget, successful comparisons with \\(u\\) or \\(v\\) force a boundary movement of at least \\(\\delta\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The endpoint assertion is Lemma 2.1. Multiplication of either budget by \\(4/5\\) multiplies \\(R\\) by that factor. If \\(R\\ge1\\) and \\(R'\\le(9/10)R\\), then\n\\[\n(9/10)^{d(R)-1}R'\n\\le\n(9/10)^{d(R)}R\n&lt;1,\n\\]\nso the integer rank decreases by at least one. Lemma 2.2 proves the reweighting assertions.</p><p>Lemmas 2.3 and 2.4 give the boundary and centered transports, including support containment, admissibility, and transfer of strict intersection-weight lower bounds.</p><p>Lemma 2.5 supplies the tightening witnesses. If the high set there has \\(\\alpha\\)-weight at most \\(\\eta k\\), then \\(u\\) qualifies for the reduced subsolution budget. Any answer \\(r\\ge u\\) satisfies \\(r_i-a_i\\ge\\delta\\) somewhere and forces a lower-bound update. Dually, if the low set has \\(\\beta\\)-weight at most \\(\\eta m\\), an answer \\(r\\le v\\) forces an upper-bound update by at least \\(\\delta\\). These witnesses depend only on the current box and protected comparison, so they can be fixed before the test call’s fresh random bits.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.1", "orig": "Lemma 3.2, “Small rank.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the box, weights, budgets, admissibility, and \\(R\\) of Proposition 2.</p><p class=\"li\">Assume \\(R&lt;1\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The vector\n\\[\np_i=\n\\begin{cases}\nb_i,&amp;\\beta_i&gt;m,\\\\\na_i,&amp;\\beta_i\\le m\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\nsatisfies \\(y\\le p\\le z\\) for every admissible subsolution \\(y\\) and supersolution \\(z\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Each summand \\(km/(\\alpha_i\\beta_i)\\) is smaller than one. Thus\n\\[\n\\alpha_i\\beta_i&gt;km:\n\\]\nthe conditions \\(\\alpha_i\\le k\\) and \\(\\beta_i\\le m\\) cannot both hold.</p><p>If \\(\\beta_i&gt;m\\), an admissible supersolution has no support at \\(i\\) and must equal \\(b_i=p_i\\), which is also at least \\(y_i\\). Otherwise \\(\\alpha_i&gt;k\\), so every admissible subsolution equals \\(a_i=p_i\\), which is at most \\(z_i\\). This includes collapsed intervals.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.2", "orig": "Lemma 3.3, “Reweighting.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the setting of Proposition 2.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(H\\subseteq V\\) satisfy \\(\\mu(H)\\le1/2\\), and keep \\([a,b]\\) fixed.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p class=\"li num\">1. Set\n   \\[\n   \\alpha_i'=\n   \\begin{cases}\n   \\alpha_i/2,&amp;i\\in H,\\\\\n   \\alpha_i,&amp;i\\notin H,\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\qquad k'=3k/5,\n   \\]\n   leaving \\(\\beta,m\\) unchanged. Every admissible subsolution with\n   \\[\n   \\alpha(S_y\\cap H)&gt;\\eta k\n   \\]\n   remains admissible, and every formerly admissible supersolution remains admissible.</p><p class=\"li num\">2. Set \\(\\beta_i'=\\beta_i/2\\) on \\(H\\), \\(\\beta_i'=\\beta_i\\) off \\(H\\), and \\(m'=3m/5\\), leaving \\(\\alpha,k\\) unchanged. Every admissible supersolution with\n   \\[\n   \\beta(S_z\\cap H)&gt;\\eta m\n   \\]\n   remains admissible, as does every formerly admissible subsolution.</p><p>In either case,\n\\[\nR'=\\frac35(1+\\mu(H))R\\le\\frac9{10}R.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>For the first change,\n\\[\n\\alpha'(S_y)\n=\n\\alpha(S_y)-\\frac12\\alpha(S_y\\cap H)\n&lt;\nk-\\frac12\\eta k\n=\n\\frac35k\n=\nk'.\n\\]\nNo supersolution parameter changes.</p><p>Write\n\\[\nA=\\sum_i\\frac1{\\alpha_i\\beta_i}.\n\\]\nHalving \\(\\alpha\\) on \\(H\\) replaces \\(A\\) by\n\\[\nA+\\sum_{i\\in H}\\frac1{\\alpha_i\\beta_i}\n=\nA(1+\\mu(H)).\n\\]\nMultiplication by the new budget product \\(3km/5\\) gives the asserted \\(R'\\).</p><p>For the second change,\n\\[\n\\beta'(S_z)\n=\n\\beta(S_z)-\\frac12\\beta(S_z\\cap H)\n&lt;\nm-\\frac12\\eta m\n=\n\\frac35m\n=\nm'.\n\\]\nThe subsolution parameters are unchanged, and the reciprocal-product sum again gains the factor \\(1+\\mu(H)\\). Finally \\(\\mu(H)\\le1/2\\) gives the rank contraction.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.3", "orig": "Lemma 3.4, “Boundary projections.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(y\\) be a subsolution and \\(z\\) a supersolution of \\([a,b]\\), for the transformed operator \\(F\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Support weights and budgets, when used, are unchanged.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p class=\"li num\">1. If \\(a\\le a'\\le b\\), then\n   \\[\n   y'=\\max(y,a')\n   \\]\n   is a subsolution of \\([a',b]\\), with\n   \\[\n   S_{y'}=\\{i:y_i&gt;a_i'\\}\\subseteq S_y.\n   \\]</p><p class=\"li num\">2. If \\(a\\le b'\\le b\\), then\n   \\[\n   z'=\\min(z,b')\n   \\]\n   is a supersolution of \\([a,b']\\), with\n   \\[\n   S_{z'}=\\{i:z_i&lt;b_i'\\}\\subseteq S_z.\n   \\]</p><p>These projections preserve admissibility. Lowering only the upper bound preserves any subsolution that still fits, with unchanged support; raising only the lower bound preserves any supersolution that still fits, with unchanged support. The new bounds need not satisfy an inequality involving \\(F\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>For the first projection, \\(a'\\le y'\\le b\\). It is active exactly where \\(y_i&gt;a_i'\\). At such a coordinate,\n\\[\ny_i'=y_i&gt;a_i'\\ge a_i,\n\\]\nand\n\\[\ny_i'=y_i\\le F_i(y)\\le F_i(y').\n\\]\nThe first inequality is the old active comparison; the second uses \\(y'\\ge y\\). At an inactive coordinate no inequality is required.</p><p>For the second projection, \\(a\\le z'\\le b'\\). Its active coordinates are exactly those with \\(z_i&lt;b_i'\\). There\n\\[\nz_i'=z_i&lt;b_i'\\le b_i,\n\\qquad\nz_i'=z_i\\ge F_i(z)\\ge F_i(z'),\n\\]\nusing \\(z'\\le z\\). Supports therefore only shrink, and positive weights preserve admissibility.</p><p>Only the lower bound defines a subsolution’s support, and only the upper bound defines a supersolution’s support. Altering the other bound affects containment alone, proving the remaining assertions, including degenerate new intervals.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.4", "orig": "Lemma 3.5, “Centered projections.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(q\\in[a,b]\\) be an integer vector and \\(A\\) a positive integer.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(y\\) be a subsolution and \\(z\\) a supersolution of \\([a,b]\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p class=\"li num\">1. If\n   \\[\n   d=\\max_i(y_i-q_i)\\ge2A,\n   \\]\n   then\n   \\[\n   y'=\\max(y-d+A,q-A)\n   \\]\n   is a subsolution of \\([q-A,q+A]\\), with\n   \\[\n   S_{y'}=\\{i:y_i-q_i&gt;d-2A\\}\\subseteq S_y.\n   \\]</p><p class=\"li num\">2. If\n   \\[\n   e=\\max_i(q_i-z_i)\\ge2A,\n   \\]\n   then\n   \\[\n   z'=\\min(z+e-A,q+A)\n   \\]\n   is a supersolution of \\([q-A,q+A]\\), with\n   \\[\n   S_{z'}=\\{i:q_i-z_i&gt;e-2A\\}\\subseteq S_z.\n   \\]</p><p>Each projection is admissible when its parent is admissible and the support weights and budget are unchanged. For every \\(H\\subseteq V\\),\n\\[\n\\alpha(S_y\\cap H)\\ge\\alpha(S_{y'}\\cap H),\n\\qquad\n\\beta(S_z\\cap H)\\ge\\beta(S_{z'}\\cap H).\n\\]\nThese conclusions do not require containment of the centered box in the parent box.</p>", "proof": "<p>The definition of \\(d\\) gives \\(y-d+A\\le q+A\\), so \\(y'\\) is in the centered box. Its active-coordinate test is\n\\[\ny_i'&gt;q_i-A\n\\iff\ny_i-q_i&gt;d-2A.\n\\]\nSince \\(d-2A\\ge0\\), an active coordinate has \\(y_i&gt;q_i\\ge a_i\\) and belongs to \\(S_y\\). There\n\\[\ny_i'\n=\ny_i-d+A\n\\le F_i(y)-d+A\n=\nF_i(y-d+A)\n\\le F_i(y').\n\\]\nThe vector \\(y-d+A\\) need not be in the parent box, but \\(F\\) is defined on all integer vectors; the old inequality is used only at an original support coordinate.</p><p>Similarly \\(z+e-A\\ge q-A\\), so \\(z'\\) belongs to the centered box. Its active test is\n\\[\nz_i'&lt;q_i+A\n\\iff\nq_i-z_i&gt;e-2A.\n\\]\nSuch a coordinate has \\(z_i&lt;q_i\\le b_i\\), and\n\\[\nz_i'\n=\nz_i+e-A\n\\ge F_i(z)+e-A\n=\nF_i(z+e-A)\n\\ge F_i(z').\n\\]\nThis proves the support formulas and comparisons. Positivity of the weights transfers admissibility and the displayed intersection bounds. With unchanged parameters, \\(\\mu(H)\\) is unchanged as well.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.5", "orig": "Lemma 3.6, “Tightening projection.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(s\\ge1\\) be an integer, \\(h=1/s\\), and \\(B\\) a reference width for \\([a,b]\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Assume \\(B&gt;128s\\), and set\n  \\[\n  \\delta=\\lfloor B/(4s)\\rfloor,\\qquad C=B-\\delta.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Then \\(\\delta&gt;0\\), and:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. If \\(y\\) is a subsolution and\n   \\[\n   d=\\max_i(y_i-a_i)&gt;C,\n   \\]\n   then\n   \\[\n   u=\\max(y-d+\\delta,a)\n   \\]\n   is a subsolution of the same box, \\(u\\le y\\), and\n   \\[\n   S_u\n   =\n   \\{i:y_i-a_i&gt;d-\\delta\\}\n   \\subseteq\n   \\{i:y_i\\ge a_i+B-hB\\},\n   \\qquad\n   \\max_i(u_i-a_i)=\\delta.\n   \\]</p><p class=\"li num\">2. If \\(z\\) is a supersolution and\n   \\[\n   e=\\max_i(b_i-z_i)&gt;C,\n   \\]\n   then\n   \\[\n   v=\\min(z+e-\\delta,b)\n   \\]\n   is a supersolution of the same box, \\(v\\ge z\\), and\n   \\[\n   S_v\n   =\n   \\{i:b_i-z_i&gt;e-\\delta\\}\n   \\subseteq\n   \\{i:z_i\\le b_i-B+hB\\},\n   \\qquad\n   \\max_i(b_i-v_i)=\\delta.\n   \\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Since \\(B/(4s)&gt;32\\), one has \\(\\delta\\ge32\\), while \\(\\delta\\le B/4\\). Hence\n\\[\nB-2\\delta\\ge B/2&gt;0,\n\\qquad\n2\\delta\\le B/(2s)\\le hB.\n\\]</p><p>For part 1 put \\(c=d-\\delta\\). The threat \\(d&gt;B-\\delta\\) implies\n\\[\nc&gt;B-2\\delta\\ge B-hB,\n\\qquad\nc&gt;0.\n\\tag{3.3}\n\\]\nThus\n\\[\na\\le u=\\max(y-c,a)\\le y\\le b.\n\\]\nA coordinate is active exactly when \\(y_i-a_i&gt;c\\). There \\(u_i=y_i-c\\), \\(y_i&gt;a_i\\), and\n\\[\nu_i=y_i-c\n\\le F_i(y)-c\n=\nF_i(y-c)\n\\le F_i(u).\n\\]\nEquation \\((3.3)\\) places every active coordinate strictly above \\(a_i+B-hB\\), hence in the asserted inclusive set; no rounding of this real threshold is used. Finally,\n\\[\nu_i-a_i\n=\n\\max\\{y_i-a_i-d+\\delta,0\\}\n\\le\\delta,\n\\]\nwith equality at a coordinate attaining \\(d\\).</p><p>For part 2 put \\(c=e-\\delta\\). Again \\(c&gt;B-2\\delta\\ge B-hB\\) and \\(c&gt;0\\), so\n\\[\na\\le z\\le v=\\min(z+c,b)\\le b.\n\\]\nIts active test is \\(b_i-z_i&gt;c\\). At an active coordinate \\(z_i&lt;b_i\\), and\n\\[\nv_i=z_i+c\n\\ge F_i(z)+c\n=\nF_i(z+c)\n\\ge F_i(v).\n\\]\nThe active coordinate lies strictly below \\(b_i-B+hB\\), and\n\\[\nb_i-v_i\n=\n\\max\\{b_i-z_i-e+\\delta,0\\}\n\\le\\delta,\n\\]\nwith equality where \\(e\\) is attained.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 3", "orig": "Proposition 4.5. The complete algorithms are Procedures 4.1–4.4", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix the graph, transformed operator \\(F\\), and \\(B_*\\) of Proposition 1.</p><p class=\"li\">Use the weights, budgets, \\(R,\\mu,d(R)\\), and admissibility of Proposition 2.</p><p class=\"li\">All vector operations are coordinatewise. Rational floors, including floors of negative values, are toward minus infinity.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix the global parameters\n  \\[\n  \\eta=\\frac45,\\qquad\n  g=1+\\lceil\\log_2(B_*+1)\\rceil,\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  s=\\min\\{2^j:j\\in\\mathbb N,\\ 2^j\\ge2^{17}(g+1)\\},\n  \\qquad h=\\frac1s,\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  J=2[s(n+1)]^6+1,\\qquad \\varepsilon=2^{-J}.\n  \\tag{4.1}\n  \\]\n  Only the analysis uses \\(\\varepsilon\\); the algorithm never constructs \\(2^J\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A permitted solver input is\n  \\[\n  (a,b;B,\\alpha,\\beta,k,m),\n  \\]\n  where \\(a,b\\in\\mathbb Z^V\\), \\(a\\le b\\), \\(1\\le B\\le B_*\\), \\(b-a\\le B\\), and all support weights and budgets are positive rationals.</p><p class=\"li\">Unmentioned parameters are inherited. A changed parameter affects only that subcall. Calls use fresh fair bits independent of pre-call history, and repetitions on a fixed input are independent.</p><p>Algorithms.</p><p class=\"li num\">1. \\(\\operatorname{Basic}(a,b)\\) is the synchronous iteration in Proposition 1.</p><p class=\"li num\">2. \\(\\operatorname{Ask}\\), originally Procedure 4.1, runs \\(J\\) independent copies of \\(\\operatorname{Solve}\\) on its given solver input and returns their coordinatewise median.</p><p class=\"li num\">3. \\(\\operatorname{OrientPlus}\\), originally Procedure 4.2, runs \\(J\\) independent copies of \\(\\operatorname{Orient}\\) on its given orientation input. It returns the majority choice in \\(\\{Y,Z\\}\\) and the concatenation of all exceptional-set lists in trial order. Minority-trial lists and duplicate entries are retained.</p><p class=\"li num\">4. \\(\\operatorname{Solve}\\), originally Procedure 4.3, computes \\(R,\\mu\\) and executes:</p><p class=\"li l2\">S1. If \\(R&lt;1\\), return \\(p_i=b_i\\) when \\(\\beta_i&gt;m\\), and \\(p_i=a_i\\) otherwise. If \\(R\\ge1\\) but \\(B\\le128s\\), return \\(\\operatorname{Basic}(a,b)\\).</p><p class=\"li l2\">S2. Set\n     \\[\n     \\delta=\\lfloor B/(4s)\\rfloor,\\qquad C=B-\\delta.\n     \\]\n     With fixed \\(B,\\alpha,\\beta,k,m\\) and changing bounds \\(a,b\\), repeat:\n     \\[\n     r=\\operatorname{Ask}(a,b;B,\\alpha,\\beta,\\eta k,m).\n     \\]\n     If \\(r_i-a_i\\ge\\delta\\) for some \\(i\\), replace the entire lower-bound vector \\(a\\) by \\(r\\) and restart. Otherwise evaluate\n     \\[\n     r=\\operatorname{Ask}(a,b;B,\\alpha,\\beta,k,\\eta m).\n     \\]\n     If \\(b_i-r_i\\ge\\delta\\) for some \\(i\\), replace the entire upper-bound vector \\(b\\) by \\(r\\) and restart. Otherwise stop with both bounds unchanged in this pass.</p><p class=\"li l2\">S3. In the final tightening box evaluate\n     \\[\n     (\\chi,\\mathcal H)\n     =\n     \\operatorname{OrientPlus}(a,b;B,\\alpha,\\beta,k,m).\n     \\]</p><p class=\"li l2\">S4. If \\(\\chi=Z\\), then for every entry \\(H\\) of \\(\\mathcal H\\), set\n     \\[\n     \\alpha_i^H=\n     \\begin{cases}\n     \\alpha_i/2,&amp;i\\in H,\\\\\n     \\alpha_i,&amp;i\\notin H,\n     \\end{cases}\n     \\qquad\n     r_H=\\operatorname{Ask}(a,b;B,\\alpha^H,\\beta,3k/5,m).\n     \\]\n     Execute the single unamplified main continuation\n     \\[\n     p=\\operatorname{Solve}(a,\\min(b,a+C);C,\\alpha,\\beta,k,m),\n     \\]\n     and return the coordinatewise maximum of \\(p\\) and all \\(r_H\\).</p><p class=\"li l2\">S5. If \\(\\chi=Y\\), then for every entry \\(H\\), set\n     \\[\n     \\beta_i^H=\n     \\begin{cases}\n     \\beta_i/2,&amp;i\\in H,\\\\\n     \\beta_i,&amp;i\\notin H,\n     \\end{cases}\n     \\qquad\n     r_H=\\operatorname{Ask}(a,b;B,\\alpha,\\beta^H,k,3m/5).\n     \\]\n     Execute\n     \\[\n     p=\\operatorname{Solve}(\\max(a,b-C),b;C,\\alpha,\\beta,k,m),\n     \\]\n     and return the coordinatewise minimum of \\(p\\) and all \\(r_H\\).</p><p>   Every listed call uses the final tightening box independently of previous listed outputs. Duplicate entries cause distinct calls. The maximum or minimum includes \\(p\\), so an empty list is permitted.</p><p class=\"li num\">5. \\(\\operatorname{Orient}\\), originally Procedure 4.4, takes a permitted box input with \\(R\\ge1\\), computes \\(\\mu\\), and executes:</p><p class=\"li l2\">O1. If \\(B\\le128s\\), compute \\(p=\\operatorname{Basic}(a,b)\\), put\n     \\[\n     P=\\{i:p_i\\ge a_i+B/2\\},\n     \\]\n     and return \\(Y\\) with \\((V\\setminus P)\\) if \\(\\mu(P)&gt;1/2\\); otherwise return \\(Z\\) with \\((P)\\).</p><p class=\"li l2\">O2. Otherwise initialize\n     \\[\n     q_0=\\left\\lfloor\\frac{a+b}{2}\\right\\rfloor,\\qquad\n     D=\\left\\lfloor\\frac{B}{32s}\\right\\rfloor,\\qquad\n     E_0=\\left\\lfloor\\frac B8\\right\\rfloor.\n     \\]\n     For \\(j=0,\\ldots,s-1\\), make independent calls\n     \\[\n     r_1=\\operatorname{Ask}(q_j-D,q_j+D;2D,\\alpha,\\beta,\\eta k,m),\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     r_2=\\operatorname{Ask}(q_j-D,q_j+D;2D,\\alpha,\\beta,k,\\eta m),\n     \\]\n     and set\n     \\[\n     q_{j+1}\n     =\n     \\operatorname{clip}_{[a,b]}\n     \\left(\n     \\left\\lfloor\\frac{r_1+r_2}{2}\\right\\rfloor\n     \\right).\n     \\]</p><p class=\"li l2\">O3. Form\n     \\[\n     P=\\{i:q_{s,i}-q_{0,i}&gt;2hB\\},\n     \\qquad\n     N=\\{i:q_{s,i}-q_{0,i}&lt;-2hB\\}.\n     \\]\n     If \\(\\mu(P)&gt;1/2\\), return \\(Y\\) and \\((V\\setminus P)\\). If that test fails but \\(\\mu(N)&gt;1/2\\), return \\(Z\\) and \\((V\\setminus N)\\).</p><p class=\"li l2\">O4. If neither test returns, draw \\(j\\) uniformly from \\(\\{0,\\ldots,s-1\\}\\), using exactly \\(\\log_2s\\) fresh bits, and evaluate\n     \\[\n     (\\chi,\\mathcal H)\n     =\n     \\operatorname{Orient}(q_j-E_0,q_j+E_0;2E_0,\\alpha,\\beta,k,m).\n     \\]\n     Return \\(\\chi\\) and the list \\(\\mathcal H\\) followed by \\(P,N\\).</p><p>   Store \\(q_0,\\ldots,q_{s-1}\\) as they are produced. Comparison cores used in the analysis are not computed.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Every permitted \\(\\operatorname{Solve}\\) execution and all its subroutine executions terminate on every random tape. Outputs of \\(\\operatorname{Solve}\\) and \\(\\operatorname{Ask}\\) are integer vectors in their respective input boxes. Outputs of \\(\\operatorname{Orient}\\) and \\(\\operatorname{OrientPlus}\\) have the stated types, and every listed set has input \\(\\mu\\)-measure at most \\(1/2\\).</p><p>Moreover:\n\\[\n\\text{tightening updates}\\le8ns,\n\\qquad\n\\text{tightening Ask calls}\\le16ns+2;\n\\]\na chain of main solver continuations has at most\n\\[\nK=8s(g+1)+1\n\\tag{4.2}\n\\]\nsolver invocations; one orientation trial visits at most \\(g+1\\) orientation invocations and returns at most \\(2g+2\\) sets; and \\(\\operatorname{OrientPlus}\\) returns at most \\(J(2g+2)\\) sets, counting repetitions.</p>", "proof": "<p>Induct on the solver rank, establishing output assertions and termination together. At rank zero, \\(R&lt;1\\), and the endpoint rule returns immediately inside the box. Fix a positive rank and assume the assertions for every smaller-rank solver input.</p><p>A budget reduction multiplies \\(R\\) by \\(4/5\\). By Proposition 2, reweighting a set with \\(\\mu(H)\\le1/2\\) multiplies it by\n\\[\n\\frac35(1+\\mu(H))\\le\\frac9{10}.\n\\]\nFor either change,\n\\[\n(9/10)^{d(R)-1}R_{\\mathrm{child}}\n\\le\n(9/10)^{d(R)}R\n&lt;1,\n\\]\nso these solver children have smaller rank. Their Ask calls terminate by induction, and coordinatewise medians remain integer vectors in their input boxes regardless of accuracy.</p><p>For orientation at the current rank, the only solver calls are budget-reduced Ask calls. At \\(B\\le128s\\), Basic terminates by Proposition 1; its returned set is either \\(P\\) of measure at most \\(1/2\\) or the complement of a set of measure greater than \\(1/2\\).</p><p>At \\(B&gt;128s\\), both \\(D\\) and \\(E_0\\) are positive. Each loop call has a legitimate centered box with reference width\n\\[\n2D\\le B/(16s).\n\\]\nBoth answers and their floored average lie in \\([q_j-D,q_j+D]\\). Since \\(q_j\\in[a,b]\\), clipping keeps \\(q_{j+1}\\) in \\([a,b]\\) and cannot increase its distance from \\(q_j\\). Hence\n\\[\n|q_{j+1,i}-q_{j,i}|\\le D.\n\\tag{4.3}\n\\]\nAll loop steps therefore terminate with valid centers.</p><p>The early-return sets have measure at most \\(1/2\\). If both tests fail, the appended \\(P,N\\) each have measure at most \\(1/2\\). The orientation child has positive reference width\n\\[\n2E_0\\le B/4\n\\]\nand unchanged support parameters, hence unchanged \\(\\mu\\). Induction on this decreasing positive integer width proves orientation termination and its measure assertion. After \\(r\\) child edges the width is at most \\(B_*/4^r\\). Since \\(g&gt;\\log_2B_*\\), there are at most \\(g\\) edges and \\(g+1\\) invocations. The terminal invocation returns one set, and each ancestor adds two, for at most \\(1+2g\\le2g+2\\) sets. Repeating \\(J\\) times gives a well-defined majority because \\(J\\) is odd, and at most \\(J(2g+2)\\) sets.</p><p>For tightening, the small-width branch is covered by Basic. Otherwise\n\\[\n\\delta=\\lfloor B/(4s)\\rfloor\\ge B/(8s)&gt;0,\n\\qquad\n0&lt;C=B-\\delta&lt;B.\n\\tag{4.4}\n\\]\nEvery output lies in the current box. A lower update only raises \\(a\\), and an upper update only lowers \\(b\\); both preserve \\(a\\le b\\). At least one coordinate moves by \\(\\delta\\). The integer quantity\n\\[\n\\sum_i(b_i-a_i)\n\\]\nstarts at most \\(nB\\) and decreases by at least \\(\\delta\\) per update. There are at most\n\\[\nnB/\\delta\\le8ns\n\\]\nupdates. A pass has at most two Ask calls and either restarts after an update or is the final pass, giving at most \\(16ns+2\\) calls.</p><p>Orientation now terminates, and every listed set satisfies the measure bound on every tape. Thus every reweighted Ask call has smaller rank and terminates. The sole main successor has a valid subbox and reference width \\(C\\). Along a chain of main successors,\n\\[\nB_{r+1}\\le B_r\\left(1-\\frac1{8s}\\right),\n\\qquad\nB_r\\le B_*\\exp\\left(-\\frac r{8s}\\right).\n\\]\nAfter \\(8s(g+1)\\) edges the latter bound would be below one, because \\(g+1&gt;\\ln B_*\\); the chain in fact stops already at width at most \\(128s\\). It has at most \\(K\\) invocations. This uses the reference width and remains valid if a cap leaves the actual box unchanged.</p><p>Work backwards along this finite chain. Reweighted outputs lie in the node’s final tightening box, and the main output lies in its subbox. Their coordinatewise maximum or minimum remains in the tightening box and therefore in the node’s input box. Backward induction proves output containment and termination at the current rank. No comparison-success event has been used.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 4", "orig": "Lemma 5.2, Proposition 5.1, and Lemma 5.6, with Lemmas 5.3–5.5 as supporting components", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the game operator, global parameters, algorithms, reference-width restrictions, rank, and admissibility of Propositions 1–3.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix an integer rank \\(r\\ge1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Assume that for every permitted solver input of rank less than \\(r\\), its output \\(p\\) satisfies, separately for every fixed admissible subsolution \\(y\\) and fixed admissible supersolution \\(z\\),\n  \\[\n  \\Pr(p\\not\\ge y)\\le\\frac1{16},\n  \\qquad\n  \\Pr(p\\not\\le z)\\le\\frac1{16}.\n  \\tag{3.1}\n  \\]\n  Each comparison is fixed before the invocation, and failure means failure in at least one coordinate.</p><p class=\"li\">For a box \\([a,b]\\) of reference width \\(B\\), define the extreme cores\n  \\[\n  I_y=\\{i:y_i\\ge a_i+B-hB\\},\n  \\qquad\n  I_z=\\{i:z_i\\le b_i-B+hB\\}.\n  \\tag{5.1}\n  \\]\n  Since \\(B&gt;0\\) and \\(h&lt;1\\), these are contained in \\(S_y,S_z\\), respectively.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p class=\"li num\">1. On any Ask input of rank less than \\(r\\), each fixed admissible comparison satisfies\n   \\[\n   \\Pr(p\\not\\ge y)\\le\\varepsilon,\n   \\qquad\n   \\Pr(p\\not\\le z)\\le\\varepsilon.\n   \\]</p><p class=\"li num\">2. On a fixed Orient input of rank \\(r\\), with output \\((\\chi,\\mathcal H)\\), every fixed admissible subsolution with \\(\\alpha(I_y)&gt;\\eta k\\) satisfies\n   \\[\n   \\Pr\\bigl(\\chi=Y\n      \\ \\text{or}\\ \n      \\exists H\\in\\mathcal H:\\alpha(S_y\\cap H)&gt;\\eta k\\bigr)\n   \\ge1-\\frac1{16}.\n   \\tag{5.2}\n   \\]\n   Separately, every fixed admissible supersolution with \\(\\beta(I_z)&gt;\\eta m\\) satisfies\n   \\[\n   \\Pr\\bigl(\\chi=Z\n      \\ \\text{or}\\ \n      \\exists H\\in\\mathcal H:\\beta(S_z\\cap H)&gt;\\eta m\\bigr)\n   \\ge1-\\frac1{16}.\n   \\tag{5.3}\n   \\]</p><p class=\"li num\">3. OrientPlus satisfies these same alternatives with failure probability at most \\(\\varepsilon\\) for each fixed qualifying comparison.</p><p>Every conclusion remains valid conditional on a pre-call history fixing its input and indicated qualifying comparison, when the call uses fresh randomness. On every tape, the orientation sets have input measure at most \\(1/2\\), and the amplified list has length at most \\(J(2g+2)\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 4.1 proves the amplified Ask assertions. Lemma 4.5 proves the single-trial orientation assertions, originally Proposition 5.1. It remains to prove amplification, originally Lemma 5.6.</p><p>Fix a qualifying \\(y\\). Failure of the amplified alternative means that the majority choice is \\(Z\\) and no set in the concatenated list has\n\\[\n\\alpha(S_y\\cap H)&gt;\\eta k.\n\\]\nEvery trial voting \\(Z\\) therefore failed its own alternative: it neither chose \\(Y\\) nor returned a qualifying set. At least \\((J+1)/2\\) trials did so. Independence, Lemma 4.5, and the subset count used for medians give\n\\[\n\\Pr(\\text{amplified failure for }y)\n\\le\n2^J(1/16)^{J/2}\n=\n\\varepsilon.\n\\]</p><p>For a fixed qualifying \\(z\\), failure means majority choice \\(Y\\) and no set with heavy intersection with \\(S_z\\). Every \\(Y\\)-voting trial failed its supersolution alternative. The same count gives \\(\\varepsilon\\).</p><p>All lists are retained, including minority lists, so absence of a witness from the concatenation implies absence from every trial list. Every entry has measure at most \\(1/2\\). Proposition 3 gives at most \\(2g+2\\) sets per trial, hence \\(J(2g+2)\\) in total, including duplicates. Conditioning on pre-call history fixes the input and comparison while leaving trial bits fresh and independent.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.1", "orig": "Lemma 5.2, “Amplified comparisons.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Assume the lower-rank solver guarantees in Proposition 4.</p><p class=\"li\">Let Ask have rank less than \\(r\\), and let \\(p\\) be its coordinatewise-median output.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For each fixed admissible subsolution \\(y\\), and separately for each fixed admissible supersolution \\(z\\),\n\\[\n\\Pr(p\\not\\ge y)\\le\\varepsilon,\n\\qquad\n\\Pr(p\\not\\le z)\\le\\varepsilon.\n\\]\nThese bounds also hold conditional on a pre-call history fixing the input and indicated admissible comparison, provided the call uses fresh randomness.</p>", "proof": "<p>Fix the input and \\(y\\). A trial is successful only when its entire output dominates \\(y\\). If a strict majority of the \\(J\\) trials succeeds, then at every coordinate a strict majority of entries is at least the corresponding coordinate of \\(y\\). The coordinatewise median therefore dominates \\(y\\).</p><p>Failure requires at least \\(t=(J+1)/2\\) unsuccessful trials. Independence and the lower-rank hypothesis bound the probability that specified \\(t\\) trials all fail by \\(16^{-t}\\). A union bound over at most \\(2^J\\) choices gives\n\\[\n\\Pr(p\\not\\ge y)\n\\le2^J16^{-t}\n\\le2^J16^{-J/2}\n=\n2^{-J}\n=\n\\varepsilon.\n\\]\nFor \\(z\\), declare a trial successful when its entire output is at most \\(z\\). A strict majority puts the median below \\(z\\) in every coordinate, and the same count applies.</p><p>Conditioning on pre-call history fixes a deterministic input and comparison. Fresh independent trial bits give the same experiment. Averaging gives the unconditional bounds. The proof uses whole-vector trial events, not independence among coordinates or simultaneous protection of different comparison vectors.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.2", "orig": "Lemma 5.3, “Center estimates.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Consider an Orient invocation with \\(B&gt;128s\\), its centers \\(q_0,\\ldots,q_s\\), and\n  \\[\n  D=\\lfloor B/(32s)\\rfloor,\\qquad E_0=\\lfloor B/8\\rfloor.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For a subsolution with \\(I_y\\ne\\varnothing\\), define\n  \\[\n  d_j=\\max_i(y_i-q_{j,i}).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For a supersolution with \\(I_z\\ne\\varnothing\\), define\n  \\[\n  e_j=\\max_i(q_{j,i}-z_i).\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>On every tape,\n\\[\nq_j\\in[a,b],\n\\qquad\n|q_{j+1,i}-q_{j,i}|\\le D,\n\\]\nand\n\\[\nD\\ge\\frac{B}{64s}&gt;0,\n\\qquad\nsD\\le E_0,\n\\qquad\n2D\\le h(2E_0).\n\\tag{5.4}\n\\]\nMoreover,\n\\[\nd_j\\ge B/2-hB-sD-1\\ge2E_0\n\\quad(0\\le j\\le s),\n\\tag{5.5}\n\\]\n\\[\nd_0-(y_i-q_{0,i})\\le hB+1\n\\quad(i\\in I_y),\n\\tag{5.6}\n\\]\nand\n\\[\ne_j\\ge B/2-hB-sD-1\\ge2E_0\n\\quad(0\\le j\\le s),\n\\tag{5.7}\n\\]\n\\[\ne_0-(q_{0,i}-z_i)\\le hB+1\n\\quad(i\\in I_z).\n\\tag{5.8}\n\\]\nCoordinate widths may be unequal or zero.</p><p>For \\(A\\in\\{D,E_0\\}\\), define\n\\[\ny^{j,A}=\\max(y-d_j+A,q_j-A),\n\\qquad\nz^{j,A}=\\min(z+e_j-A,q_j+A).\n\\tag{5.9}\n\\]\nThese are comparisons in \\([q_j-A,q_j+A]\\), with exact supports\n\\[\nS_{y^{j,A}}\n=\n\\{i:y_i-q_{j,i}&gt;d_j-2A\\}\n\\subseteq S_y,\n\\]\n\\[\nS_{z^{j,A}}\n=\n\\{i:q_{j,i}-z_i&gt;e_j-2A\\}\n\\subseteq S_z.\n\\tag{5.10}\n\\]\nThey preserve admissibility under unchanged support parameters.</p>", "proof": "<p>Proposition 3 puts both answers at time \\(j\\), and their floored average, in \\([q_j-D,q_j+D]\\). Since \\(q_j\\in[a,b]\\), clipping cannot move a coordinate farther from \\(q_j\\). Starting at \\(q_0\\in[a,b]\\) proves containment and the step bound on every tape.</p><p>Since \\(B/(32s)&gt;4\\), its floor is at least half its value. Also,\n\\[\nsD\\le B/32\\le B/8-1\\le E_0.\n\\]\nHere \\(B&gt;128s\\) and \\(s\\ge16\\) justify the middle inequality. Dividing \\(sD\\le E_0\\) by \\(s\\) gives the final inequality of \\((5.4)\\).</p><p>Write \\(\\ell_i=b_i-a_i\\), so \\(0\\le\\ell_i\\le B\\), and\n\\[\nq_{0,i}=a_i+\\lfloor\\ell_i/2\\rfloor.\n\\]\nFor a subsolution, \\(y_i\\le b_i\\) gives\n\\[\nd_0\\le\\max_i\\lceil\\ell_i/2\\rceil\\le\\lceil B/2\\rceil.\n\\]\nFor \\(i\\in I_y\\),\n\\[\ny_i-q_{0,i}\n\\ge B-hB-\\lfloor\\ell_i/2\\rfloor\n\\ge B/2-hB.\n\\]\nThe difference is at most \\(hB+1/2\\), proving \\((5.6)\\). Each coordinate moves by at most \\(sD\\) during the loop. Keeping any one high-core coordinate in the maximum gives\n\\[\nd_j\\ge B/2-hB-sD,\n\\]\nand therefore the first inequality of \\((5.5)\\).</p><p>For the supersolution,\n\\[\ne_0\\le\\max_i\\lfloor\\ell_i/2\\rfloor\\le\\lfloor B/2\\rfloor.\n\\]\nAt \\(i\\in I_z\\),\n\\[\nq_{0,i}-z_i\n\\ge B-hB-\\lceil\\ell_i/2\\rceil\n\\ge B/2-hB-\\frac12.\n\\]\nThis proves \\((5.8)\\); subtracting the possible movement \\(sD\\) proves the first inequality in \\((5.7)\\).</p><p>Using \\(sD\\le B/32\\) and \\(2E_0\\le B/4\\),\n\\[\n(B/2-hB-sD-1)-2E_0\n\\ge\nB\\left(\\frac7{32}-\\frac1s\\right)-1\n&gt;0.\n\\]\nThe last inequality follows from \\(s\\ge16\\) and \\(B&gt;128s\\). Thus both deviations are large enough for the centered projections in Proposition 2, at both radii \\(D,E_0\\). That proposition gives \\((5.9)\\), \\((5.10)\\), and admissibility. The centered box need not lie inside \\([a,b]\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.3", "orig": "Lemma 5.4, “Deviation drops.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Assume the lower-rank solver guarantees of Proposition 4.</p><p class=\"li\">Use the centers and deviations of Lemma 4.2.</p><p class=\"li\">A time \\(j\\) is light for the fixed subsolution \\(y\\) when\n  \\[\n  \\alpha\\bigl(\\{i:y_i-q_{j,i}&gt;d_j-2D\\}\\bigr)\\le\\eta k.\n  \\tag{5.11}\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">A time \\(j\\) is light for the fixed supersolution \\(z\\) when\n  \\[\n  \\beta\\bigl(\\{i:q_{j,i}-z_i&gt;e_j-2D\\}\\bigr)\\le\\eta m.\n  \\tag{5.12}\n  \\]\n  Each condition is determined before the two calls at time \\(j\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For each fixed admissible subsolution with nonempty high core, with probability at least \\(1-2s\\varepsilon\\), every loop step satisfies\n\\[\nd_{j+1}\\le d_j,\n\\qquad\nd_{j+1}\\le d_j-D\n\\quad\\text{at every light time for }y.\n\\]\nSeparately, for each fixed admissible supersolution with nonempty low core, with probability at least \\(1-2s\\varepsilon\\), every step satisfies\n\\[\ne_{j+1}\\le e_j,\n\\qquad\ne_{j+1}\\le e_j-D\n\\quad\\text{at every light time for }z.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>For \\(y\\), condition on the history before time \\(j\\). The projection \\(y^{j,D}\\) is fixed and admissible for the second call, which retains subsolution budget \\(k\\) and reduces \\(m\\). Lemma 4.1 gives, with conditional error at most \\(\\varepsilon\\),\n\\[\nr_2\\ge y^{j,D}\\ge y-d_j+D.\n\\]\nWithout any accuracy assumption,\n\\[\nr_1\\ge q_j-D\\ge y-d_j-D.\n\\]\nTheir average is at least the integer vector \\(y-d_j\\); flooring preserves this lower bound. Upper clipping preserves it because \\(b\\ge y\\ge y-d_j\\), and lower clipping can only help. Thus\n\\[\nq_{j+1}\\ge y-d_j,\n\\]\nor \\(d_{j+1}\\le d_j\\).</p><p>At a light time, the exact support formula also makes \\(y^{j,D}\\) admissible for the first call, with subsolution budget \\(\\eta k\\). With one further conditional error at most \\(\\varepsilon\\), both answers are at least \\(y-d_j+D\\). Flooring preserves this integer bound, and\n\\[\nd_j\\ge2E_0\\ge2D\n\\]\nensures \\(y-d_j+D\\le y\\le b\\), so clipping preserves it. Hence \\(d_{j+1}\\le d_j-D\\).</p><p>For \\(z\\), the first call retains the full supersolution budget. Except with conditional probability \\(\\varepsilon\\),\n\\[\nr_1\\le z^{j,D}\\le z+e_j-D.\n\\]\nThe second answer always satisfies\n\\[\nr_2\\le q_j+D\\le z+e_j+D.\n\\]\nTheir average, and then its floor, are at most \\(z+e_j\\). Lower clipping preserves this because \\(a\\le z\\le z+e_j\\), while upper clipping can only help. Thus \\(e_{j+1}\\le e_j\\).</p><p>At a light time for \\(z\\), the second call also admits \\(z^{j,D}\\). With one additional conditional error at most \\(\\varepsilon\\), both answers are at most \\(z+e_j-D\\). Since \\(e_j\\ge2D\\), this bound is at least \\(z\\ge a\\), so flooring and clipping preserve it. Hence \\(e_{j+1}\\le e_j-D\\).</p><p>For either fixed side, at most two amplified comparisons are requested per step. The projection and qualification are determined before the appropriate fresh call. Summing the conditional errors over \\(s\\) steps gives \\(2s\\varepsilon\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.4", "orig": "Lemma 5.5, “Core displacement.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the fixed comparisons and events of Lemma 4.3.</p><p class=\"li\">Let\n  \\[\n  P=\\{i:q_{s,i}-q_{0,i}&gt;2hB\\},\n  \\qquad\n  N=\\{i:q_{s,i}-q_{0,i}&lt;-2hB\\}.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>On the subsolution event of Lemma 4.3,\n\\[\nI_y\\cap N=\\varnothing;\n\\]\nif there are more than \\(256\\) light times for \\(y\\), then \\(I_y\\subseteq P\\).</p><p>On the separate supersolution event,\n\\[\nI_z\\cap P=\\varnothing;\n\\]\nmore than \\(256\\) light times for \\(z\\) imply \\(I_z\\subseteq N\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>For \\(i\\in I_y\\), Lemma 4.2 gives\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nq_{s,i}-q_{0,i}\n&amp;\\ge y_i-d_s-q_{0,i}\\\\\n&amp;=(d_0-d_s)-\\bigl(d_0-(y_i-q_{0,i})\\bigr)\\\\\n&amp;\\ge(d_0-d_s)-(hB+1).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nOn the stipulated event \\(d_0-d_s\\ge0\\). Since \\(hB=B/s&gt;128\\), this is greater than \\(-2hB\\), excluding \\(N\\).</p><p>More than \\(256\\) light times means at least \\(257\\) drops:\n\\[\nd_0-d_s\\ge257D\\ge\\frac{257}{64}hB.\n\\]\nConsequently,\n\\[\nq_{s,i}-q_{0,i}-2hB\n\\ge\n\\left(\\frac{257}{64}-3\\right)hB-1\n=\n\\frac{65}{64}hB-1\n&gt;0,\n\\]\nso \\(i\\in P\\).</p><p>For \\(i\\in I_z\\), similarly\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nq_{s,i}-q_{0,i}\n&amp;\\le z_i+e_s-q_{0,i}\\\\\n&amp;=\\bigl(e_0-(q_{0,i}-z_i)\\bigr)-(e_0-e_s)\\\\\n&amp;\\le(hB+1)-(e_0-e_s).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nNonincrease of \\(e_j\\) makes this strictly less than \\(2hB\\), excluding \\(P\\). With at least \\(257\\) light times,\n\\[\ne_0-e_s\\ge257D,\n\\]\nand\n\\[\nq_{s,i}-q_{0,i}+2hB\n\\le1-\\frac{65}{64}hB&lt;0.\n\\]\nThus \\(i\\in N\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.5", "orig": "Proposition 5.1, “Orientation.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Assume the lower-rank solver guarantees of Proposition 4.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix a permitted Orient input of rank \\(r\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Fix an admissible subsolution \\(y\\) with \\(\\alpha(I_y)&gt;\\eta k\\), or, in a separate assertion, an admissible supersolution \\(z\\) with \\(\\beta(I_z)&gt;\\eta m\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  \\gamma=\\frac{256}{s}+2s\\varepsilon,\n  \\]\n  and\n  \\[\n  \\lambda(B)=\n  \\begin{cases}\n  0,&amp;B\\le128s,\\\\\n  1+\\lambda(2\\lfloor B/8\\rfloor),&amp;B&gt;128s.\n  \\end{cases}\n  \\]\n  Since child width is at most \\(B/4\\), \\(\\lambda(B)\\le g+1\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For the output \\((\\chi,\\mathcal H)\\) of one Orient trial, the subsolution alternative \\((5.2)\\), and separately the supersolution alternative \\((5.3)\\), have failure probability at most\n\\[\n\\lambda(B)\\gamma&lt;\\frac1{16}.\n\\]\nEvery listed set has input measure at most \\(1/2\\) on every tape. The probability assertions hold conditional on a pre-call history fixing the input and qualifying comparison.</p>", "proof": "<p>Proceed by induction on the reference width, separately for the two fixed comparisons.</p><p>At \\(B\\le128s\\), Orient computes the exact box solution \\(p\\). Proposition 1 gives \\(y\\le p\\le z\\). With\n\\[\nP=\\{i:p_i\\ge a_i+B/2\\},\n\\]\na high-core coordinate satisfies\n\\[\np_i\\ge y_i\\ge a_i+(1-h)B&gt;a_i+B/2,\n\\]\nso \\(I_y\\subseteq P\\). A low-core coordinate satisfies\n\\[\np_i\\le z_i\\le b_i-B+hB\\le a_i+hB&lt;a_i+B/2,\n\\]\nso \\(I_z\\subseteq V\\setminus P\\).</p><p>If \\(\\mu(P)&gt;1/2\\), choice \\(Y\\) protects the subsolution and the returned set \\(V\\setminus P\\) contains the low core. Otherwise choice \\(Z\\) protects the supersolution and the returned set \\(P\\) contains the high core. Core containment in support transfers the strict weight bound. Both alternatives hold with zero error, and the returned set has measure at most \\(1/2\\).</p><p>Now let \\(B&gt;128s\\) and fix \\(y\\) with \\(\\alpha(I_y)&gt;\\eta k\\). Its core is nonempty. Charge error at most \\(2s\\varepsilon\\) for failure of Lemma 4.3, and work on the complementary event.</p><p>If \\(\\mu(P)&gt;1/2\\), Orient returns \\(Y\\), proving the alternative. If it returns at the second test, \\(\\mu(N)&gt;1/2\\), it supplies \\(V\\setminus N\\). Lemma 4.4 gives \\(I_y\\cap N=\\varnothing\\), so\n\\[\n\\alpha(S_y\\cap(V\\setminus N))\n\\ge\\alpha(I_y)&gt;\\eta k.\n\\]\nIf neither test returns, both \\(P,N\\) have measure at most \\(1/2\\). With more than \\(256\\) light times, Lemma 4.4 gives \\(I_y\\subseteq P\\), so the appended set \\(P\\) proves the alternative regardless of the child.</p><p>It remains to consider successful-loop histories with at most \\(256\\) light times. Fix the completed loop history. The sampled index is drawn only now, with fresh bits; its probability of being light is at most \\(256/s\\). This also holds on histories where all required loop comparisons succeeded, since that event is determined before sampling.</p><p>For a sampled nonlight index put\n\\[\nT_y=\\{i:y_i-q_{j,i}&gt;d_j-2D\\},\n\\]\n\\[\nB'=2E_0,\\qquad\na'=q_j-E_0,\\qquad b'=q_j+E_0,\n\\]\nand use the child comparison\n\\[\ny'=y^{j,E_0}\n=\n\\max(y-d_j+E_0,q_j-E_0).\n\\]\nIt is admissible and \\(S_{y'}\\subseteq S_y\\). Nonlightness gives \\(\\alpha(T_y)&gt;\\eta k\\). For \\(i\\in T_y\\),\n\\[\ny_i'\n\\ge y_i-d_j+E_0\n&gt;\nq_{j,i}+E_0-2D\n\\ge\nq_{j,i}+E_0-hB'\n=\na_i'+B'-hB',\n\\]\nusing \\(2D\\le hB'\\). Thus \\(T_y\\) lies in the child’s high core and \\(\\alpha(I_{y'})&gt;\\eta k\\).</p><p>The loop history and index fix the child input and comparison before the child’s fresh bits. Width induction applies conditionally, with failure at most \\(\\lambda(B')\\gamma\\). A successful child returns \\(Y\\), copied by the parent, or a retained set \\(H\\) with\n\\[\n\\alpha(S_{y'}\\cap H)&gt;\\eta k.\n\\]\nSupport containment gives \\(\\alpha(S_y\\cap H)&gt;\\eta k\\). The total failure bound is\n\\[\n2s\\varepsilon+\\frac{256}{s}+\\lambda(B')\\gamma\n=\n\\lambda(B)\\gamma.\n\\]</p><p>For the separate supersolution argument, fix \\(z\\) with \\(\\beta(I_z)&gt;\\eta m\\). Outside error at most \\(2s\\varepsilon\\), Lemma 4.4 gives \\(I_z\\cap P=\\varnothing\\). A first-test return of \\(Y\\) supplies \\(V\\setminus P\\), containing \\(I_z\\), so its support intersection has weight greater than \\(\\eta m\\). A second-test return chooses \\(Z\\), proving the alternative directly.</p><p>If neither returns, more than \\(256\\) light times give \\(I_z\\subseteq N\\), so the appended set \\(N\\) proves the alternative. Otherwise the fresh index is light with probability at most \\(256/s\\). For a nonlight index put\n\\[\nT_z=\\{i:q_{j,i}-z_i&gt;e_j-2D\\},\n\\]\n\\[\nz'=z^{j,E_0}\n=\n\\min(z+e_j-E_0,q_j+E_0).\n\\]\nThen \\(\\beta(T_z)&gt;\\eta m\\), \\(z'\\) is admissible in \\([a',b']\\), and \\(S_{z'}\\subseteq S_z\\). For \\(i\\in T_z\\),\n\\[\nz_i'\n\\le z_i+e_j-E_0\n&lt;\nq_{j,i}-E_0+2D\n\\le\nq_{j,i}-E_0+hB'\n=\nb_i'-B'+hB'.\n\\]\nHence \\(T_z\\) lies in the child’s low core. Conditional width induction gives choice \\(Z\\) or a retained set \\(H\\) with \\(\\beta(S_{z'}\\cap H)&gt;\\eta m\\), and support containment transfers the latter to \\(S_z\\). The same total error bound follows.</p><p>The measure assertion is independent of comparison success. Early returns use a set of measure at most \\(1/2\\), or the complement of a set of measure greater than \\(1/2\\). Recursive returns append only \\(P,N\\) of measure at most \\(1/2\\). Child support parameters, and hence \\(\\mu\\), are unchanged.</p><p>Finally,\n\\[\n\\lambda(B)\\gamma\n\\le\n(g+1)\\left(\\frac{256}{s}+2s2^{-J}\\right)\n\\le\n\\frac1{512}+J2^{-J}\n&lt;\n\\frac1{16}.\n\\]\nIndeed \\(s\\ge2^{17}(g+1)\\), while\n\\[\n2s(g+1)\\le s^2\\le J.\n\\]\nFor \\(J\\ge16\\),\n\\[\nJ2^{-J}\\le16\\cdot2^{-16}=\\frac1{4096}.\n\\]</p><p>Every probabilistic step fixes its input and one qualified comparison before fresh bits. The same argument applies after fixing any pre-call history that determines them.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 5", "orig": "Theorem 6.3, “Pointwise comparisons for the solver.”", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix the transformed game operator \\(F\\), global parameters, and algorithms of Propositions 1 and 3.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\([a,b]\\) be any finite integer box with positive integer reference width \\(B\\le B_*\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\alpha,\\beta\\in\\mathbb Q_{&gt;0}^V\\) and \\(k,m\\in\\mathbb Q_{&gt;0}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Admissibility is as in Proposition 2:\n  \\[\n  \\alpha(S_y)\\le k,\\qquad \\beta(S_z)\\le m.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(p\\) be the output of\n  \\[\n  \\operatorname{Solve}(a,b;B,\\alpha,\\beta,k,m).\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every fixed admissible subsolution \\(y\\), and separately for every fixed admissible supersolution \\(z\\),\n\\[\n\\Pr(p\\ge y)\\ge1-\\frac1{16},\n\\qquad\n\\Pr(p\\le z)\\ge1-\\frac1{16}.\n\\]\nThese are whole-vector inequalities: the comparison is chosen before the invocation, and its inequality holds at every coordinate on its success event. No simultaneous guarantee for all admissible comparisons in one execution is asserted.</p>", "proof": "<p>Induct on \\(d(R)\\), allowing every permitted box and all positive rational support parameters at each rank. Rank zero means \\(R&lt;1\\), and Proposition 2 proves both comparisons without error. At any rank the branch \\(B\\le128s\\) returns the exact box solution, which Proposition 1 places above every subsolution and below every supersolution.</p><p>Fix a positive rank and assume the assertions at smaller ranks. Budget reductions and permitted reweightings decrease rank, so Proposition 4 and the conditional estimates in Lemmas 5.2–5.3 are available. Orientation correctness has already been obtained by a separate width induction using only smaller-rank solver guarantees.</p><p>Fix an admissible subsolution before the invocation. Follow its obligation along the actual main chain using Lemma 5.3. At entry to each node, the box and comparison are functions of completed computations. Until the first charged error, Lemma 5.2 preserves admissibility and increases the protected vector. The local work then either completes the obligation or passes one admissible comparison to the main successor. In the latter case analyze that successor’s local work next, rather than invoking a same-rank guarantee.</p><p>Proposition 3 bounds the chain by\n\\[\nK=8s(g+1)+1\n\\]\nnodes. Combining the tightening charge with \\((6.4)\\), the charge at one reached node is at most \\(\\Lambda\\varepsilon\\), where\n\\[\n\\Lambda=16ns+3+J(2g+2)\n\\le100s(n+1)+10J(g+2).\n\\tag{6.5}\n\\]\nThese conditional bounds apply on histories reaching the node without an earlier error, because those histories retain the comparison invariants. Lemma 5.1 bounds the probability of a first error along the chain by \\(K\\Lambda\\varepsilon\\).</p><p>If no error occurs, start at the node completing the obligation, or at the exact terminal solver if the obligation reaches the end. Its output has the required inequality. Work backwards: each preceding node has its required local inequalities, and its main successor supplies the passed comparison. Its output therefore dominates its protected vector. Tightening projections dominate the entry comparison, so the inequality propagates to the initial comparison. A main call still executed below a node that completed the obligation is irrelevant to this implication.</p><p>For a fixed supersolution, follow the separate upper-comparison obligations. Tightening decreases the protected vector, passed comparisons remain admissible, and the same backward argument gives the original upper bound. The request count is again \\(\\Lambda\\). No event simultaneous over all comparison vectors is used.</p><p>For the numerical bound put\n\\[\nQ=s(n+1).\n\\]\nThe global choices give \\(Q\\ge2\\), \\(g+2\\le s\\le Q\\), and\n\\[\nJ=2Q^6+1\\ge32.\n\\]\nIn particular,\n\\[\nK\\le9Q^2,\n\\qquad\n\\Lambda\\le100Q+10J(g+2)\\le20JQ,\n\\]\nusing \\(J\\ge10\\). Since \\(Q^3\\le J\\),\n\\[\nK\\Lambda\\varepsilon\n\\le180JQ^3\\,2^{-J}\n\\le180J^2\\,2^{-J}\n&lt;\n\\frac1{16}.\n\\tag{6.6}\n\\]\nIndeed \\(J^22^{-J}\\) decreases for integer \\(J\\ge3\\), and\n\\[\n180\\cdot32^2\\,2^{-32}&lt;1/16.\n\\]\nThis closes both pointwise rank inductions.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.1", "orig": "Lemma 6.1, “Comparisons determined by the past.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Suppose a randomized procedure has failure probability at most \\(\\rho\\) for every fixed input and every fixed comparison satisfying a specified qualification.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mathscr H\\) denote the history before a call.</p><p class=\"li\">Assume the input, proposed comparison, and its qualification are determined by \\(\\mathscr H\\), and that the call uses fresh randomness.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\Pr(\\text{qualification holds and comparison fails}\\mid\\mathscr H)\n\\le\n\\rho\\,\\mathbf1_{\\{\\text{qualification holds}\\}}.\n\\]\nThe bound remains usable after restricting to any event determined by the pre-call history.</p><p>Consequently, if an analysis requests at most \\(M\\) comparisons, stops requesting after its first failure, and each qualified request has conditional failure probability at most \\(\\rho\\), then the probability of a first failure is at most \\(M\\rho\\), provided every request and qualification is fixed before its call.</p>", "proof": "<p>Condition on a possible history. If the comparison does not qualify, the event is empty. Otherwise the history specifies a deterministic eligible input and comparison. Fresh bits retain their original distribution independently of the history, so the pointwise bound applies. Averaging gives the assertion, including restriction to a pre-call event.</p><p>For the first-error consequence, reaching a specified request with no earlier failure is a pre-call event. Apply the conditional bound there and sum over the \\(M\\) possible positions, treating unreached positions as empty events. Only one comparison is required at each position.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.2", "orig": "Lemma 6.2, “Tightening a fixed comparison.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix a solver rank and assume the pointwise comparison guarantees of Proposition 5 at every smaller rank.</p><p class=\"li\">Consider a Solve tightening loop with \\(B&gt;128s\\), and set\n  \\[\n  \\delta=\\lfloor B/(4s)\\rfloor,\\qquad C=B-\\delta.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Fix an admissible subsolution \\(y^{\\mathrm{in}}\\) before the loop begins; separately, fix an admissible supersolution \\(z^{\\mathrm{in}}\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Outside an event of probability at most \\((16ns+2)\\varepsilon\\), the final box contains an admissible subsolution \\(y\\ge y^{\\mathrm{in}}\\), obtained by successive lower-bound projections, such that\n\\[\n\\max_i(y_i-a_i)&gt;C\n\\Longrightarrow\n\\alpha(\\{i:y_i\\ge a_i+B-hB\\})&gt;\\eta k.\n\\tag{6.1}\n\\]\nSeparately, outside an event with the same bound, the final box contains an admissible supersolution \\(z\\le z^{\\mathrm{in}}\\) satisfying\n\\[\n\\max_i(b_i-z_i)&gt;C\n\\Longrightarrow\n\\beta(\\{i:z_i\\le b_i-B+hB\\})&gt;\\eta m.\n\\tag{6.2}\n\\]\nBoth assertions hold conditionally on preceding history determining the loop input and initial comparison.</p>", "proof": "<p>Specify at most one comparison to request from each actual Ask call. The analysis stops at its first failed request; the algorithm continues its prescribed execution.</p><p>For the subsolution, let \\(y\\) be the current protected projection. Before a reduced-\\(k\\) call, the box, \\(y\\), and\n\\[\nd=\\max_i(y_i-a_i)\n\\]\nare determined. If \\(d&gt;C\\), form\n\\[\nu=\\max(y-d+\\delta,a).\n\\]\nProposition 2 makes this a subsolution with support contained in\n\\[\nI_y=\\{i:y_i\\ge a_i+B-hB\\}.\n\\]\nIndeed, an active coordinate satisfies\n\\[\ny_i-a_i&gt;d-\\delta&gt;B-2\\delta\\ge B-hB.\n\\]\nAt a coordinate attaining \\(d\\), \\(u_i=a_i+\\delta\\). If \\(\\alpha(S_u)\\le\\eta k\\), request that the call return an answer at least \\(u\\). This comparison and qualification are fixed before the call; success forces a lower-bound update. If \\(d\\le C\\), or \\(u\\) does not qualify, make no request.</p><p>Whenever the algorithm raises \\(a\\) to an output \\(r\\), replace \\(y\\) by \\(\\max(y,r)\\). Both vectors are below the unchanged upper bound. The boundary projection in Proposition 2 preserves admissibility, does not increase support, and does not decrease coordinates. It requires only the unconditional fact that \\(r\\) lies in the old box.</p><p>At every reached reduced-\\(m\\) call request comparison with the current \\(y\\). Its full subsolution parameters are unchanged, so it qualifies. Success gives \\(r\\ge y\\); lowering the upper bound to \\(r\\), if an update occurs, retains \\(y\\) and leaves its support and inequalities unchanged.</p><p>In the final pass neither bound changes. Its reduced-\\(k\\) call uses the final box and protected vector. If \\(d&gt;C\\) but \\(\\alpha(I_y)\\le\\eta k\\), then \\(u\\) qualifies because \\(S_u\\subseteq I_y\\); absent a charged failure, its output forces an update, a contradiction. This proves \\((6.1)\\). Identifying the final pass afterwards makes no new probabilistic demand.</p><p>For the supersolution, let \\(z\\) denote the protected vector. At every reduced-\\(k\\) call request an output at most \\(z\\). The full supersolution parameters are unchanged, and success retains \\(z\\) if the lower bound rises.</p><p>Before a reduced-\\(m\\) call put\n\\[\ne=\\max_i(b_i-z_i).\n\\]\nIf \\(e&gt;C\\), define\n\\[\nv=\\min(z+e-\\delta,b).\n\\]\nProposition 2 makes \\(v\\) a supersolution supported in\n\\[\nI_z=\\{i:z_i\\le b_i-B+hB\\},\n\\]\nbecause an active coordinate has\n\\[\nb_i-z_i&gt;e-\\delta&gt;B-2\\delta\\ge B-hB.\n\\]\nA coordinate attaining \\(e\\) has \\(v_i=b_i-\\delta\\). When \\(v\\) qualifies for the reduced budget, request an output at most \\(v\\); success forces an upper update. After any upper update to \\(r\\), project \\(z\\) to \\(\\min(z,r)\\), preserving admissibility and not increasing support. At the final pass, \\(e&gt;C\\) together with \\(\\beta(I_z)\\le\\eta m\\) would force an update, proving \\((6.2)\\).</p><p>Until the first error, every requested comparison qualifies and is fixed before its call. Proposition 3 bounds the loop by \\(16ns+2\\) Ask calls. Under the smaller-rank hypothesis, Proposition 4 gives conditional error \\(\\varepsilon\\) for each request. Lemma 5.1 gives total error \\((16ns+2)\\varepsilon\\). Conditioning first on the history before the loop gives the conditional form.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.3", "orig": "Section 6.3, the unnumbered local comparison-propagation argument", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Assume the pointwise guarantees at every smaller solver rank.</p><p class=\"li\">Consider a nonterminal Solve node after successful tightening, with final box \\([a,b]\\), parameters \\(B,\\alpha,\\beta,k,m\\), and \\(C=B-\\lfloor B/(4s)\\rfloor\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Fix the resulting admissible subsolution \\(y\\), satisfying \\((6.1)\\), or separately the resulting admissible supersolution \\(z\\), satisfying \\((6.2)\\), before the orientation call.</p><p class=\"li\">The node returns a coordinatewise maximum for choice \\(Z\\) and a coordinatewise minimum for choice \\(Y\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For either fixed side, local comparisons can be requested so that, outside conditional error at most\n\\[\n\\bigl(1+J(2g+2)\\bigr)\\varepsilon,\n\\tag{6.4}\n\\]\neither:</p><p class=\"li\">a reweighted local output establishes the required comparison for the node’s returned vector independently of its main output; or</p><p class=\"li\">one admissible comparison is passed to the main successor, and satisfaction of that passed comparison, together with the requested local comparisons, implies the required comparison at the node.</p><p>All requested comparisons and qualifications are determined before their corresponding fresh calls. No correctness guarantee for the same-rank main successor is assumed.</p>", "proof": "<p>For a subsolution \\(y\\), request the OrientPlus alternative exactly when\n\\[\n\\max_i(y_i-a_i)&gt;C.\n\\]\nEquation \\((6.1)\\) supplies the core qualification. Proposition 4 bounds conditional failure by \\(\\varepsilon\\). No orientation guarantee is needed when the maximum is at most \\(C\\).</p><p>If the choice is \\(Y\\), the node returns a minimum. Reweighted calls change only supersolution parameters, so \\(y\\) qualifies in every one; request a lower comparison with \\(y\\) from each. Put\n\\[\na'=\\max(a,b-C),\n\\qquad\ny'=\\max(y,a').\n\\]\nProposition 2 makes \\(y'\\) an admissible subsolution in the main box \\([a',b]\\). If the main output is at least \\(y'\\) and all local outputs are at least \\(y\\), their minimum is at least \\(y\\). Pass \\(y'\\) to the main successor.</p><p>If the choice is \\(Z\\), put\n\\[\nb'=\\min(b,a+C).\n\\]\nIf \\(y\\le b'\\), the same \\(y\\) remains admissible in \\([a,b']\\); a main output at least \\(y\\) controls the returned maximum. Pass \\(y\\), requesting no reweighted comparisons.</p><p>If instead \\(y\\not\\le b'\\), then \\(y\\le b\\) implies \\(\\max_i(y_i-a_i)&gt;C\\). Successful orientation supplies a listed set \\(H\\) with\n\\[\n\\alpha(S_y\\cap H)&gt;\\eta k.\n\\tag{6.3}\n\\]\nSelect the first such entry for the analysis. Proposition 2 makes \\(y\\) admissible in its reweighted call. Request a lower comparison there. Success makes the returned maximum at least \\(y\\), whatever the main output; the obligation ends at this node.</p><p>The selected witness is a function of the completed list and the vector fixed before orientation. Its index is therefore determined before any reweighted call begins. The algorithm executes every entry without computing this analysis-dependent index. Earlier entries may have returned before the selected entry, but the selected call’s bits remain fresh.</p><p>For a supersolution \\(z\\), request orientation exactly when\n\\[\n\\max_i(b_i-z_i)&gt;C,\n\\]\nusing \\((6.2)\\).</p><p>For choice \\(Z\\), all reweighted calls retain the supersolution parameters. Request an upper comparison with \\(z\\) from every entry. Put\n\\[\nb'=\\min(b,a+C),\n\\qquad\nz'=\\min(z,b').\n\\]\nThen \\(z'\\) is admissible in \\([a,b']\\). If the main output is at most \\(z'\\) and every reweighted output is at most \\(z\\), their maximum is at most \\(z\\). Pass \\(z'\\).</p><p>For choice \\(Y\\), put\n\\[\na'=\\max(a,b-C).\n\\]\nIf \\(z\\ge a'\\), it remains admissible in \\([a',b]\\), and a main output at most \\(z\\) controls the returned minimum; pass \\(z\\). If \\(z\\not\\ge a'\\), then \\(z\\ge a\\) implies \\(\\max_i(b_i-z_i)&gt;C\\). Successful orientation supplies an entry \\(H\\) with\n\\[\n\\beta(S_z\\cap H)&gt;\\eta m.\n\\]\nSelect its first occurrence before the calls. Proposition 2 makes \\(z\\) admissible there. Its successful upper comparison forces the returned minimum below \\(z\\), independently of the main output.</p><p>Thus the minimum for a subsolution under \\(Y\\), and the maximum for a supersolution under \\(Z\\), require all specified reweighted comparisons; witness cases require one. Proposition 3 bounds entries by \\(J(2g+2)\\) on every tape. Proposition 4 and Lemma 5.1 charge at most \\((1+J(2g+2))\\varepsilon\\), including the possible orientation request. Internal calls of OrientPlus are already included in its guarantee and are not charged again.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 6", "orig": "Corollary 6.4, “Exact recovery of the top box solution.”", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix the transformed game operator and \\(B_*=8nW\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(x\\) be the unique solution of \\([0,B_*]\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let\n  \\[\n  p=\\operatorname{Solve}(0,B_*;B_*,\\mathbf1,\\mathbf1,n,n),\n  \\]\n  where scalar box endpoints denote constant vectors.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\Pr(p=x)\\ge\\frac78.\n\\]\nConsequently,\n\\[\n\\{i:p_i&gt;B_*/2\\}\n\\]\nis the complete zero-threshold winning set with probability at least \\(7/8\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The deterministic vector \\(x\\) is both a subsolution and a supersolution. Each support has cardinality at most \\(n\\), so it qualifies on both sides for the unit weights and budgets \\(n\\).</p><p>Apply Proposition 5 to this same vector in both roles and to the same output \\(p\\):\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Pr(p=x)\n&amp;=\\Pr(p\\ge x\\text{ and }p\\le x)\\\\\n&amp;\\ge1-\\Pr(p\\not\\ge x)-\\Pr(p\\not\\le x)\\\\\n&amp;\\ge1-\\frac1{16}-\\frac1{16}\n=\\frac78.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThe intersection is equality of whole vectors; independence is unnecessary. Proposition 1 identifies the thresholded output on this event.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 7", "orig": "Theorem 7.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the complete algorithms of Proposition 3 on the root input\n  \\[\n  [0,B_*],\\qquad\n  \\alpha=\\beta=\\mathbf1,\\qquad k=m=n,\\qquad B=B_*.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(L\\) be the explicit binary input length and set \\(x=L+2\\).</p><p class=\"li\">The bit estimates concern parameters reachable from this root, although Proposition 5 permits arbitrary positive rational parameters.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a uniform implementation in a standard bit-operation model and an absolute constant \\(C&gt;0\\) such that, for every input and every random tape, the total running time is at most\n\\[\n2^{C(\\log_2(L+2))^2}.\n\\]\nThe bound includes preprocessing, all recursive executions, intermediate arithmetic, and random-bit generation.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 7.1 bounds the number of solver invocations. Lemma 7.2 controls operands even in centered boxes extending beyond their parents. Lemma 7.3 bounds all local work, terminal iterations, storage manipulation, and random-bit generation.</p><p>Let \\(T_d\\) bound total bit cost for a solver invocation of rank at most \\(d\\), including descendants. Contracting its main chain as in Lemma 7.1 gives\n\\[\nT_0\\le P(L),\n\\qquad\nT_d\\le KP(L)+KA_0T_{d-1}\n\\quad(d\\ge1).\n\\tag{7.8}\n\\]\nWith \\(K=O(x^2)\\), \\(KA_0=O(x^{28})\\), and \\(D_*=O(\\log x)\\), this yields\n\\[\nT_{D_*}\n=\n2^{O((\\log_2(L+2))^2)}.\n\\]</p><p>A uniform machine performs every instruction on binary strings. Polynomial simulation overhead for data access or recursion in a chosen standard model changes only the absolute constant in the exponent. The resulting bound includes preprocessing, arithmetic, all recursive executions, storage manipulation, random bits, and final thresholding, on every tape.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.1", "orig": "Section 7, counting calls at one rank and contracting main chains", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the root and notation of Proposition 7.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(D_*=d(n^3)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  A_0\n  =\n  (16ns+2)J+\n  \\bigl[2s(g+1)+2g+2\\bigr]J^2.\n  \\tag{7.3}\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(N_d\\) bound the total number of solver invocations generated by one invocation of rank at most \\(d\\), including itself, for the fixed graph and global parameters.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\log_2(B_*+1)=O(x),\\qquad\ng=O(x),\\qquad\ns=O(x),\\qquad\nJ=O(x^{12}),\n\\tag{7.1}\n\\]\nand\n\\[\nD_*\n=\n1+\\left\\lfloor\\log_{10/9}(n^3)\\right\\rfloor\n=\nO(\\log(n+1))\n=\nO(\\log x).\n\\tag{7.2}\n\\]\nMoreover,\n\\[\nN_0=1,\n\\qquad\nN_d\\le K+KA_0N_{d-1}\\quad(d\\ge1),\n\\tag{7.4}\n\\]\nso\n\\[\nN_{D_*}=2^{O((\\log_2x)^2)}\n\\tag{7.5}\n\\]\non every tape.</p>", "proof": "<p>Explicit encoding gives \\(n=O(x)\\), \\(|E|=O(x)\\), and at most \\(L\\) bits for any input weight. The transformation\n\\[\nt(e)=(n+1)w(e)+1\n\\]\nand the definitions of \\(W,B_*\\) give \\(\\log_2(B_*+1)=O(x)\\), hence \\(g=O(x)\\). The least-power-of-two choice gives\n\\[\ns&lt;2^{18}(g+1),\n\\]\nso \\(s=O(x)\\) and \\(J=O(x^{12})\\). These are binary-length bounds even when magnitudes are exponential.</p><p>Initially\n\\[\nR=km\\sum_i\\frac1{\\alpha_i\\beta_i}=n^3.\n\\]\nNo descendant increases it. Its maximum rank is therefore \\((7.2)\\); the strict rank convention gives \\(D_*=1\\) at \\(n=1\\).</p><p>At one node of a main Solve chain, expose Solve calls inside amplification and orientation, leaving smaller-rank solver invocations unexpanded:</p><p class=\"li\">Tightening contributes at most \\((16ns+2)J\\) solver invocations.</p><p class=\"li\">OrientPlus performs \\(J\\) trials, each with at most \\(g+1\\) levels, at most \\(2s\\) Ask calls per level, and \\(J\\) solver invocations per Ask. This contributes\n  \\[\n  2s(g+1)J^2.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">The exceptional list has at most \\(J(2g+2)\\) entries. Each causes an Ask containing \\(J\\) solver invocations, contributing\n  \\[\n  (2g+2)J^2.\n  \\]</p><p>Their sum is \\(A_0\\). Both quadratic amplification terms are counted, including minority lists and duplicate entries. All these calls have smaller rank. In addition, a nonterminal node has exactly one unamplified main continuation.</p><p>Contract the main chain, whose length is at most \\(K\\) by Proposition 3. Each of its nodes has at most \\(A_0\\) immediately smaller-rank solver descendants. This gives \\((7.4)\\). The single unamplified main successor is what permits this contraction.</p><p>From \\((7.1)\\),\n\\[\nK=O(x^2),\\qquad\nA_0=O(x^{26}),\\qquad\nKA_0=O(x^{28}).\n\\]\nWith \\(A=KA_0\\), induction gives\n\\[\nN_d\\le(K+1)(A+1)^d.\n\\]\nSince \\(D_*=O(\\log x)\\), equation \\((7.5)\\) follows. Every count is deterministic and holds on every tape.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.2", "orig": "Section 7, integer magnitudes and exact rational parameters", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the root computation of Proposition 7.</p><p class=\"li\">Consider any ancestry path in the full subroutine tree.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(D_*=d(n^3)\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Every actual endpoint, center, and returned coordinate belongs to the real interval\n\\[\n[-B_*/6,\\,7B_*/6].\n\\tag{7.6}\n\\]\nAll intermediate integers used for midpoints, differences, scalar shifts, caps, and evaluations of \\(F\\) have \\(O(\\log_2(B_*+1))=O(x)\\) bits.</p><p>Every reachable support weight has the form\n\\[\n\\alpha_i=2^{-u_i^\\alpha},\n\\qquad\n\\beta_i=2^{-u_i^\\beta},\n\\qquad\nu_i^\\alpha,u_i^\\beta\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0},\n\\qquad\nu_i^\\alpha+u_i^\\beta\\le D_*.\n\\]\nReachable budgets have the forms\n\\[\nk=n\\frac{4^{u_k}3^{v_k}}{5^{u_k+v_k}},\n\\qquad\nm=n\\frac{4^{u_m}3^{v_m}}{5^{u_m+v_m}},\n\\]\n\\[\nu_k+v_k+u_m+v_m\\le D_*.\n\\tag{7.7}\n\\]\nTheir numerators and denominators have \\(O(\\log(n+1))\\) bits. Support sums, reciprocal-product sums, rank tests, measure tests, budget tests, and height-threshold tests admit exact polynomial-bit-cost implementations.</p>", "proof": "<p>All child boxes are contained in their parents except possibly centered boxes\n\\[\n[q-A,q+A],\n\\qquad A\\in\\{D,E_0\\},\n\\]\nformed by Orient. Their centers belong to the parent box, their radii satisfy \\(A\\le B/8\\), and their reference widths satisfy \\(2A\\le B/4\\). All other transitions preserve or decrease reference width.</p><p>List the radii of centered transitions along an ancestry as \\(A_1,A_2,\\ldots\\). The first is at most \\(B_*/8\\). After radius \\(A_r\\), reference width is \\(2A_r\\), and cannot increase before the next centered transition. Hence\n\\[\nA_{r+1}\\le(2A_r)/8=A_r/4.\n\\]\nThe possible outward movement of either endpoint is at most\n\\[\n\\sum_{r\\ge1}A_r\n\\le\n\\frac{B_*}{8}\\sum_{r\\ge0}4^{-r}\n=\n\\frac{B_*}{6}.\n\\]\nStarting from \\([0,B_*]\\) proves \\((7.6)\\). These are real bounds on integer values; no rounding is intended.</p><p>Temporary midpoint sums, differences, scalar shifts, and caps have magnitude \\(O(B_*)\\). Since \\(|t(e)|\\le W\\le B_*\\), values \\(t(e)+p_j\\) do as well. Thus all have \\(O(x)\\) bits, independently of comparison outcomes.</p><p>Weights and budgets change only on parameter-reducing edges into solver calls. There are at most \\(D_*\\) such edges on an ancestry. Starting from unit weights and budgets \\(n\\), this gives the displayed forms and \\(O(\\log(n+1))\\)-bit numerators and denominators. Store reduced binary fractions or the explicit exponents.</p><p>Support sums admit a common power-of-two denominator of exponent at most \\(D_*\\). For \\(S\\subseteq V\\),\n\\[\n\\sum_{i\\in S}\\frac1{\\alpha_i\\beta_i}\n=\n\\sum_{i\\in S}2^{u_i^\\alpha+u_i^\\beta}\n\\le n2^{D_*}.\n\\]\nThese sums have \\(O(\\log(n+1))\\) bits. Exact integer arithmetic computes them and tests \\(R&lt;1\\) or \\(\\mu(S)&gt;1/2\\). Budget and height-threshold comparisons are exact after multiplying by positive denominators. Alternatively, normalization by the Euclidean algorithm after each operation has polynomial bit cost.</p><p>The global \\(g,s\\) are computed by binary lengths and comparisons with powers of two. Neither ranks nor real logarithms used in the analysis need evaluation. The number \\(2^{-J}\\) is only an error bound and is not constructed.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.3", "orig": "Section 7, local work, random bits, and implementation", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the root computation and bit representations of Proposition 7 and Lemma 7.2.</p><p class=\"li\">Attach to a Solve node all local work in its wrappers and orientation routines, leaving its descendant Solve calls unexpanded.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The attached work is bounded by a polynomial \\(P(L)\\) of absolute degree, uniformly on every tape. This includes terminal Basic iterations in orientation, storage and manipulation of all centers and exceptional lists, and generation of random index bits.</p><p>The complete routines are uniform finite binary computations. Under fair randomness, successive subcalls receive fresh independent bits from the unread tape tail.</p>", "proof": "<p>Basic is called only at \\(B\\le128s\\). Proposition 1 bounds it by\n\\[\n1+128ns\n\\]\noperator evaluations. Each scans the explicit edge list, adds and compares \\(O(x)\\)-bit integers, and clips. The count is independent of endpoint offsets and numerical weight magnitudes. The other terminal branch \\(R&lt;1\\) has no iteration. Neither incurs work proportional to \\(W\\) or \\(B_*\\).</p><p>Store a vertex subset as an \\(n\\)-bit incidence vector. Compute a coordinatewise median by sorting the \\(J\\) entries in each coordinate and selecting entry \\((J+1)/2\\). Majority votes, concatenation, clipping, floored averages, and extrema use polynomially many operations on the bounded-size values.</p><p>One main-chain node has at most \\(J(g+1)\\) orientation invocations and polynomially many local vector-loop steps. Their terminal Basic calls contribute at most\n\\[\nJ(g+1)(1+128ns)\n\\]\noperator evaluations; these are not additional solver invocations in \\(N_d\\). Storing every center and exceptional-list entry still takes polynomial local data and work. This proves a polynomial bound \\(P(L)\\).</p><p>The only random primitive samples an iterate index. Since \\(s\\) is a power of two, exactly \\(\\log_2s\\) fair bits select it uniformly, without rejection. The \\(J\\) trials are sequential and take fresh bits from one tape. Proposition 3 ensures every preceding execution terminates on every tape. Under fair randomness, the unread tail after this finite stopping time supplies independent fresh bits. On an arbitrary fixed tape, all deterministic call bounds remain valid. Each orientation invocation reads at most \\(\\log_2s\\) bits of its own, already covered locally.</p><p>Ask and OrientPlus perform their specified finite numbers of calls. All other instructions are binary integer and rational computations. The final winning-set output takes \\(n\\) threshold comparisons; preprocessing and this output are absorbed by enlarging \\(P\\).</p>"}], "edges": [[0, 1, "cite"], [3, 0, "cite"], [3, 1, "cite"], [3, 7, "cite"], [3, 13, "cite"], [3, 14, "cite"], [3, 19, "cite"], [3, 20, "cite"], [3, 24, "cite"], [3, 28, "cite"], [4, 3, "cite"], [4, 5, "cite"], [5, 3, "cite"], [5, 6, "cite"], [6, 3, "cite"], [7, 13, "cite"], [7, 14, "cite"], [7, 16, "cite"], [7, 20, "cite"], [7, 22, "cite"], [7, 23, "cite"], [8, 7, "cite"], [9, 7, "cite"], [10, 7, "cite"], [11, 7, "cite"], [12, 7, "cite"], [13, 0, "cite"], [13, 14, "cite"], [13, 16, "cite"], [13, 20, "cite"], [13, 22, "cite"], [13, 23, "cite"], [13, 25, "cite"], [13, 26, "cite"], [13, 28, "cite"], [14, 20, "cite"], [14, 22, "cite"], [14, 23, "cite"], [15, 14, "cite"], [15, 17, "cite"], [16, 17, "cite"], [16, 18, "cite"], [17, 18, "cite"], [17, 19, "cite"], [18, 19, "cite"], [19, 14, "cite"], [20, 24, "cite"], [20, 25, "cite"], [21, 20, "cite"], [21, 22, "cite"], [21, 23, "cite"], [22, 20, "cite"], [23, 20, "cite"], [24, 0, "cite"], [24, 1, "cite"], [25, 0, "cite"], [25, 1, "cite"], [26, 25, "cite"], [27, 25, "cite"], [27, 28, "cite"], [28, 25, "cite"]]});
